\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen hits 4-yr high vs dollar, BOJ seen restrained.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 31 (Reuters) - The yen jumped over 1.5 percent against the dollar to four-year highs on Wednesday on optimism over the Japanese economy and a perception Japan is using less firepower to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose at breakneck speed after hurdling 105 yen per dollar, previously considered a line in the sand for Japanese authorities, which have spent 30 trillion yen in the past year in an attempt to curb the yen's potentially export-damaging strength.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, rose to one-week highs against the dollar as investors scaled back expectations for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "When the 105 level broke, which the BOJ spent so much money defending in February, that was perceived to be a strong signal that Japan will tolerate a yen appreciation," said Adam Cole, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations for an ECB rate cut are now much reduced, and euro/dollar has benefited from that."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said although the BOJ was suspected of selling yen for dollars in Tokyo hours, the size appeared relatively small compared to recent massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also finding support from expectations of an upbeat quarterly tankan survey at 2350 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as far as 103.78 yen by 0740 GMT, down 1.68 percent from the U.S. close and on track for its largest one-day loss since December 2002.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also fell more than one percent to hit four-month lows of 127.07 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose over half a percent against the dollar to trade at $1.2245, slightly below earlier one-week highs but a rise of two cents from 2004 lows hit on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro and safe-haven Swiss franc also gained against the dollar in Tokyo trade on news that explosions and fire had rocked BP Plc's refinery complex in Texas City, Texas. The FBI had last week warned of a security threat to refiners in that area, but a BP spokeswoman said there was no sign of outside influence.  \par \par \uc2 TANKAN SUPPORT  \par \par \uc2 Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment is expected to reinforce the patchy signs of economic recovery seen in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll forecast the survey would reveal heightened optimism among big manufacturers, benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand.  \par \par \uc2 The market was also looking to figures for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, which are due at 1000 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the Finance Ministry's international bureau, said the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month was slightly above the 3.3420 trillion yen Japan sold in February.  \par \par \uc2 Investors continued to be wary about further BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new fiscal year gets underway.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to believe they're going to let dollar/yen go into freefall and go below 100. The line in the sand at 105 has gone, clearly, but I wouldn't be surprised to see another one above 100," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London.  \par \par \uc2 ECB IN FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 Traders were looking ahead to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on Thursday, with a consensus building that the ECB will leave rates alone.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. non-farm payroll figures for March due on Friday are expected to show that U.S. companies are hiring more aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone economic sentiment data and flash HICP inflation for March are due at 0900 GMT, with inflation expected to show a rise of 1.6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The Chicago purchasing managers' index, due at 1500 GMT, is forecast to show a fall to 61.5 in March from 63.6 in February.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve Board Governor Edward Gramlich speaks on budget and trade deficits at 2025 GMT in Los Angeles.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v0011i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
