\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan loses battle to keep yen below 105 per dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 827 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 31 (Reuters) - Japan lost a battle on Wednesday to stop the yen from strengthening beyond 105 per dollar, a level that authorities had seemed determined to defend and which big exporters have said is crucial for their competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 Despite repeated, aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past year, the yen has strengthened because of a recovering economy and a rising stock market, as well as concern about geopolitical risks that have undermined the dollar against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As speculation grew that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be scaled back in the new fiscal year starting on Thursday, the yen finally broke through the perceived line of defence.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totalled 4.706 trillion yen ($45.28 billion) over the last month, up from 3.342 trillion yen in the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 That was the third largest monthly volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on record, but traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have slowed in the last two weeks and is likely to be less aggressive in the coming months as the economy recovers.  \par \par \uc2 "With recent economic indicators coming in strong and Japanese share prices rising, the authorities may be thinking they don't need to be particular about the 105 yen level," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The 105 level has long been seen as crucial for officials worried that a strong yen could derail the export-led economic recovery that is taking hold after a decade of stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF has spent more than 35 trillion yen ($337 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last year.  \par \par \uc2 Many Japanese exporters such as electronics giant Sony Corp (6758.T) have made 105 yen the basis of their planning for the 2004/05 business year.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been criticised by its trade partners, who say that keeping the yen artificially low gives Japan an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki on Wednesday denied that the government planned to change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Our basic stance has not changed - that it is desirable for exchange rates to reflect fundamentals. When that is not the case we will do what we have to do," Tanigaki told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 At a later news conference, however, he said that Japan's economy had grown more resilient to a stronger yen - a remark that added to doubts about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily.  \par \par \uc2 "Tanigaki's comments were worth taking note of because he said explicitly that Japan can put up with a stronger yen," said Bhanu Baweja, strategist with UBS in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 END OF BUSINESS YEAR  \par \par \uc2 A senior MOF official also denied that the end of the fiscal year had any relevance for the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "As we have been saying repeatedly, the idea that we will change our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after a certain point in time is merely speculation," the official told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 However, persistent talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can not continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the recent pace has fuelled the yen's latest rise, which accelerated after the 105 level was broken. The yen rose as high as 103.40 to the dollar, its highest since April 2000.  \par \par \uc2 That was a gain of more than eight percent from a trough near 112.35 just three weeks ago, which was reached after an aggressive round of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that prevented the yen from breaching the 105 barrier in an earlier attempt last month.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also up 14 percent from around 118 to the dollar where it finished on March 31 last year.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday, traders said, but that could not hold back yen bulls, spurred by expectations that the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" corporate survey to be released on Thursday would show a further recovery in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The report is expected to show that the export-led recovery in the manufacturing sector is reaching non-manufacturers, underscoring the view that the economy may have reached a stage where a strong yen is no longer such a big risk to growth.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's gross domestic product expanded at its fastest pace in 13 years in the final three months of last year, clocking growth of 6.4 percent on an annualised basis in real terms.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei share average briefly fell after the yen's surge but bounced back to close 0.19 percent higher on the day at 11,715.39 - a gain of 47 percent for the business year.  \par \par \uc2 Criticism of the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been growing in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a heated exchange in the Lower House on Wednesday, Democrat Fumihiko Igarashi called the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy "stupid" and likened it to a dependence on narcotics.  \par \par \uc2 "This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is stupid, because it's a fight we can't win," Igarashi said.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=105.81 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v000z0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
