\par\par \b \uc2 Yen at nearly 4-year peak, dollar hurt by BP blast.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 31 (Reuters) - The yen climbed against the dollar to its highest level in nearly four years on Wednesday, boosted by fiscal year-end flows, optimism over the Japanese economy and a fire at a Texas refinery.   \par \par \uc2 The euro also rose to its highest level in a week and sterling was modestly up against the dollar on news that explosions and fire had rocked BP Plc's refinery complex in Texas City, Texas.   \par \par \uc2 The FBI had last week warned of a security threat to refiners in that area, but a BP spokeswoman said there was no sign of outside influence.   \par \par \uc2 News of the fire gave Asian markets another excuse to push the yen higher, past the two big figures 105 and 104 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 It rose as far as 103.97 yen per dollar its highest since June 2000, before retreating slightly on suspicions that the Bank of Japan was selling the unit.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was yielding 104.40/45 yen at 0525 GMT, while the euro was around $1.2220   \par \par \uc2 Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo, said the yen's rise could have been due to bunched dollar sales by Japanese exporters on the last day of the financial year in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "There is no strong sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And, given the strong economic fundamentals, the movement is natural," he said.   \par \par \uc2 He estimated the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to smooth the yen's moves if it firmed too fast, a moderation from the aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen in the past on which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent 30 trillion yen ($283.5 billion) since last year.   \par \par \uc2 TANKAN SUPPORT   \par \par \uc2 Markets had not expected the dollar/yen rate to fall much at the fiscal year-end, as a weaker yen was seen boosting valuations of Japanese foreign assets.   \par \par \uc2 The yen was also supported by hope that Japan's "tankan" survey of business sentiment on Thursday would reinforce the patchy signs of economic recovery seen in recent months.   \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll forecast the quarterly survey would reveal heightened optimism among big manufacturers, benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand.   \par \par \uc2 At Wednesday's highs, the yen has risen over seven percent versus the dollar from an early-March low near 112.30 and nearly three percent this year. Economists say it is still undervalued in trade-weighted terms.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, markets braced for some volatility in the dollar/yen pair given expectations that U.S. non-farm payroll figures due on Friday will show that U.S. companies are hiring more aggressively.   \par \par \uc2 "I think we'll see some yen buying on the tankan figures, but as long as Friday's U.S. jobs data comes in at around the market consensus or above we'll see the dollar settle or even make gains," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.   \par \par \uc2 The market was also looking to figures for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, which are due at 1000 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the Finance Ministry's international bureau, said the volume of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month was slightly above the 3.3420 trillion yen Japan sold in February.   \par \par \uc2 EURO BENEFIT MUTED   \par \par \uc2 Markets also bought the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  giving up over a yen from the day's peaks to hit 127.40 yen   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the euro was probably sidelined ahead of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on Thursday, with a consensus building that the ECB will leave rates alone.   \par \par \uc2 But the deeper reason behind the euro's nearly six percent decline from record highs in February, analysts said, was a shift in portfolio funds as American investors exited positions in commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , high-yielders, euro and sterling to make good their equity and bond losses at home.   \par \par \uc2 "I am not a buyer of a structural downtrend in the euro, I think it is more flows-related than the return of the strength of the dollar," said Lee Boon Keng, a strategist with DBS Bank in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "And, I don't think the dollar can trade dichotomously between the euro and yen for a prolonged period of time. The euro has to appreciate too."   \par \par \uc2 Michael Jansen, markets strategist with the National Australia Bank in Sydney, differed.   \par \par \uc2 He said the markets' view seemed to be that the euro had peaked for this year. Besides, with the BOJ more likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  through dollar buying, speculators would be more comfortable buying the yen for euros, he said. ($1=105.81 yen) (Reporting by Vidya Ranganathan; Editing by Brent Kininmont; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging: vidya.ranganathan.reuters.com@reuters.net; +813 3432 8656)).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v000mr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
