\par\par \b \uc2 Yen creeps up vs dollar ahead of tankan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 31 (Reuters) - The yen edged up against the dollar on Wednesday on high expectations for a key report on Japanese business sentiment and as markets awaited a euro zone decision on interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The market was keenly awaiting Japan's "tankan" survey of business sentiment on Thursday, and a policy meeting on the same day by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a consensus building that the ECB will leave rates alone.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, non-farm payroll figures due on Friday are expected to show that U.S. companies are hiring more aggressively, raising expectations that the Federal Reserve will move quicker to lift rates from four-decade lows.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we'll see some yen buying on the tankan figures, but as long as Friday's U.S. jobs data comes in at around the market consensus or above we'll see the dollar settle or even make gains," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0115 GMT, the dollar was fetching 105.45 yen below its late New York level of 105.70 and just off a 3-= year low of around 105.16 hit last month.  \par \par \uc2 The closely watched tankan business survey is expected to show further improvement in business sentiment in Japan, particularly among big manufacturers benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand.  \par \par \uc2 An upbeat survey would suggest the economy can withstand a stronger exchange rate, putting even more question marks over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry is due to announce at 1000 GMT how much it spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from February 26 to March 29.  \par \par \uc2 The market is expecting a figure of anywhere between 3.5 and 5.0 trillion yen. That would compare with the 3.342 trillion yen ($31.58 billion) spent in February.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar rose as high as 105.91 yen, gaining some support from buying by importers ahead of commercial fixing on the last day of the fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen almost 12 percent against the yen over that period, as foreign investors snapped up Japanese stocks, helping the Nikkei stock average surge around 47 percent after three straight years of bruising losses.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts predict the dollar's fall against the yen to continue in the year ahead, given that a recent raft of positive data on Japan's economy is helping fuel a rally in Japanese shares and increasing demand for the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was around $1.2180 slightly up from $1.2165 in late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were also looking to euro zone data later in the day, which is expected to show consumer prices rose 1.6 percent year-on-year in March, unchanged from February.  \par \par \uc2 Other data is expected to show that economic sentiment in the euro zone remained stable at 96.00 in March, the same as February. ($1=105.81 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
