\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ FOCUS: Analysts Say Dollar/Yen May Dip More But Hinge On MOF  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,297 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -- Reduced BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Stronger Japanese Economy May Help Yen \par   -- BOJ Not Expected To Completely Abstain From \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Markets \par   -- Short-Covering Dollar Bounce Possible In New Japanese Fiscal Year \par  \par By Allen Sykora \par     Chicago, March 31 (OsterDowJones) - The U.S. dollar could weaken some more \par against the Japanese yen in the first months of Japan's new fiscal year, \par particularly if some of the recent reports prove to be true and the Bank of \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  curtails dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts say. \par     Still, analysts are talking about a decline of only a few yen, rather than \par a massive sell-off. \par     For one thing, chart support for dollar/yen lies only a few yen from where \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair currently trades, a technician pointed out. One analyst \par suggested dollar/yen could even end up bouncing a little bit in the \par foreseeable future because of covering on all of the short dollar/yen \par positions. \par     Furthermore, most analysts say that even if the BOJ does cut back on its \par dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts, they don't expect monetary authorities to \par completely give up either, especially if their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to \par strengthen and thus threaten the country's export-oriented economy. \par     On the final day of Japan's fiscal year, the greenback has plunged as far \par as 103.39 yen, its weakest level since April 2000. \par     Dollar/yen has been undercut by such factors as a softer-than-forecast \par Chicago Purchasing Managers Index report and unsubstantiated rumors about the \par health of Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, said David Solin, partner \par with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics. \par     "The weak Chicago PMI caught everyone by surprise," he said. "It's \par interesting in that everyone is waiting for the big bounce-back in the labor \par market, and as people are waiting, the (U.S.) economy has been deteriorating, \par although not anything super negative." \par     A key factor undercutting dollar/yen lately has been the apparent \par curtailment of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the dollar, a number of \par contacts emphasized. \par     Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it spent 4.7 trillion yen on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par between Feb. 26 and March 29, the third-highest monthly amount on record. \par     However, much of that appeared to have occurred during the earlier part of \par the month, pointed out Bob Kozak, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  futures analyst with Alaron. \par     In the last couple of weeks, several newspapers have suggested the BOJ may \par curtail those efforts in the new fiscal year, which begins Thursday. In fact, \par it's already become apparent that the BOJ is cutting back, sources said. \par     "Presumably, the passing of Japan's fiscal year-end has led to a \par diminished effort from the BOJ, although I wouldn't say that they've backed \par away from the market completely," said Bob Lynch, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with BNP \par Paribas. "They're not announcing anything, so it's hard to tell from day to \par day. But there appears to be a significant decline in the amount of \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." \par     In the new fiscal year, he said, "we think the yen is going to strengthen \par further. The economic recovery in Japan appears to be taking firmer hold. And \par while there is still likely to be some resistance among Japan's monetary \par authorities to too much or too fast appreciation of the yen, a stronger \par economy should leave them a bit more tolerant of yen strength." \par     In fact, he added, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki indicated \par such last night. \par     Lynch pointed out that Japan might be hurt by a muscular yen less than in \par the past since so much of its manufacturing has already been moved elsewhere, \par including a number of auto plants now operated in the U.S. \par     Furthermore, said Lynch and Kozak, if Japan's economy improves, there \par could be flows of foreign cash into the country's financial markets, further \par supporting the yen. \par     Lynch said his firm currently looks for dollar/yen to fall to around 102 \par yen around the end of the second quarter. Kozak suggested a dollar/yen level \par around 101 to 102. \par     "It really depends on what the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is going to do," said \par Kozak. \par  \par     SHORT-COVERING DOLLAR/YEN BOUNCE POSSIBLE \par  \par     Some analysts, such as Solin, say dollar/yen might actually bounce on \par short covering to 107 or 108 yen in the next month or so. \par     In the last couple of years, he said, there has been a rush by traders to \par short dollar/yen on expectations of repatriation flows into Japan ahead of the \par March 31 end of the country's fiscal year. \par     "The flip side is you've also seen some pretty good bottoms around this \par time," Solin continued. "You've seen a lot of people shorting it in front of \par the fiscal end of the year and then covering once we get into April. There is \par some chance of that happening again over the next couple of weeks. \par     "We're down eight big figures (yen) in the last couple of weeks," he \par said. "So what often happens after those kinds of moves is a period of \par consolidation." \par  \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CURTAILED BUT NOT OVER \par  \par     Analysts say it's not realistic for Japanese authorities to continue \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as aggressively on dollar/yen as they were earlier this year. \par     "The amounts, in the runup through February, were just massive and \par unsustainable," said Solin. \par     Echoed Lynch: "It's unreasonable to expect them to continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par at the pace they had been in January and February when - depending on which \par exchange rate you use - they had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by somewhere around $100 billion. \par     "The G-7 (Group of Seven) reaffirmed at the last two meetings they want \par market forces to dictate exchange rates. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in those kinds of amounts \par doesn't really coincide with the G-7's stated objective. So I assume part of \par the reason they would want to cut back (on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) is to avoid criticism \par from the G-7." \par     BOJ officials might also start worrying about the financial risk they \par would be undertaking over time if they continue to amass dollar reserves, he \par added. \par     Still, most analysts doubt Japanese authorities will give up on \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  completely, also. \par     "I think what you will see is them in on occasion more for smoothing and \par to say 'we're here,'" said Solin. \par     Kozak suggested Japanese officials are likely in the process of \par reassessing how they will proceed on dollar/yen. \par     "I don't think they have abandoned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said. "I think they \par will still use it as a tool to stand behind their exporters, should they see \par the market is taking a hit and the yen goes higher." \par     Kozak pointed out that Japan's exporters are in competition with China. \par     "If they feel they can't compete with Chinese exports, they have to do \par something," he continued. "If it means starting some type of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par periodically, they may do that." \par     Kozak added that some of Finance Minister Tanigaki's overnight comments \par could be construed as verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as when he suggested \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy has not changed and officials would act if the market does not \par reflect fundamentals. \par  \par     DOLLAR/YEN NOT FAR FROM LONGTIME SUPPORT \par  \par     Technically, right around the end of 1999 and beginning of 2000, \par dollar/yen held around the 101.25-yen area, pointed out Andrew Chaveriat, \par technical analyst with BNP Paribas. \par     "I think there is a good chance we might hold above this 101 area," said \par the analyst, pointing out this was a level where the BOJ defended the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par aggressively a few years ago. \par     Decent channel support on a weekly chart lies only a few more yen below \par this, around 97 to 98 yen, he added. \par  \par   --- \par Allen Sykora, OsterDowJones \par sykora@osterdowjones.com \par Copyright 2004 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-31-04 1359ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020040331e03v000lz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
