\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Not Yet Ready To Ditch  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 968 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Having confirmed its outlay in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the first three months of the year was 75% of last year's record total, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped aside and let the dollar tumble to a four-year low against the yen Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 This doesn't mean \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has turned its back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely though, despite the growing belief that it's resigned to accepting yen strength as a reflection of its improving economy.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, given the massive scale of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort so far this year, it's highly unlikely the finance ministry will suddenly abandon this policy, analysts say. After all, selling yen in the market isn't aimed solely at keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nominally weak for the benefit of Japanese exporters. With interest rates in Japan almost zero, it's also a monetary policy tool designed to wipe out deflation and stimulate consumer spending. Both goals have yet to really be achieved.  \par \par \uc2 With the market building up short dollar positions - dangerously large bets on further dollar depreciation against the yen - the climate could be ripe for a short-squeeze, especially if it helps prevent the dollar breaking below key psychological support at Y100.00. A short squeeze would happen if the dollar rose sharply, forcing many short sellers to buy it back to cover their positions and limit losses. That buying would force up the value of the curency further.  \par \par \uc2 "We think (the Japanese monetary authorities) would be more aggressive in preventing a (dollar) free-fall than the overnight market action would suggest," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital. He expects Japan's monetary authorities will defend Y100.00 as best they can.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the last day of Japan's fiscal year, the dollar fell as low as Y103.44 - a level not seen since April 2000 - as official bids from the Bank of Japan, which acts on behalf of the government, failed to materialize.  \par \par \uc2 The finance ministry also said Wednesday that it spent Y4.7 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the Feb. 26-March 29 period, the third highest monthly amount ever. This brings the total so far this year to a staggering Y15.2 trillion, three quarters of last year's record Y20.05 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 Early afternoon Wednesday, the dollar had recovered to Y104.25, still over a full yen weaker however, than Y105.64 late Tuesday in New York. This recovery was aided in large part by the euro's recovery against the yen - it's EURO strengthening across the board - to Y128.20 from a low of Y126.56 earlier Wednesday. But this is still a touch weaker than late Tuesday's Y128.56.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Rhetoric Remains The Same \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That Y4.7 trillion wasn't enough to avert yen appreciation - the dollar fell to Y105.27 on Mar. 29 from around Y109.00 on Feb. 26 - highlights the weight of onflows into Japan and strength of real demand for yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Nikkei stock market index and broader Topix index rose 47% and 50%, respectively, in the year to March 31. And portfolio managers are still beefing up their exposure to Japanese equities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strength in Japanese equities and broader economic conditions, backed up by the dollar's 7.5% decline from over Y112 in less than a month, has led many analysts to suggest Japan is more tolerant of a stronger yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But if the rhetoric is to be believed, that's not necessarily the case. Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reaffirmed Wednesday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy is unchanged and said authorities "will take necessary action" if exchange rates move too sharply. He also said Tokyo will leave \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates up to the market if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  move in a way that reflects underlying economic fundamental factors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One Japanese bank executive said he reckons the finance ministry regards intraday moves of about two and a half yen as excessive, which would warrant action. This suggests official bids could be lying around Y103.20, he said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists at HSBC pointed out in a research note that Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi has even weighed into the debate, saying exchange rates must be watched closely. "That sort of comment could slow the move to Y101.00," they wrote.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And as Neil Jones, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at Nomura International in London notes, "verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is often followed up by actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki dismissed suggestions from Japanese lawmakers that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the second half of March was scaled back because of pressure from U.S. officials. Treasury Secretary John Snow and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan both intimated that Japan's yen-selling was unsustainable in such massive amounts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think that picture is correct," Tanigaki said, adding that Greenspan's comments weren't referring specifically to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels or policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki's heavy hints that the finance ministry hasn't abandoned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seem to be falling on deaf ears in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, which is building up large long yen and short dollar positions. Having slammed speculators earlier this month who had bet heavily on a rising dollar by pulling official bids, the ministry could again be tempted to wipe out the speculative element from the yen's exchange rates  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market's too short (of dollars) for my taste," said Neal Kimberly, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-31-04 1438ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040331e03v0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\pics