\par\par \b \uc2 MONUMENT SECURITIES: More Bad News For US Treasuries  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,070 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 13:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Stephen Lewis \par    Of Monument Securities \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--High oil prices used to be bad news for the yen. Today, however, reports of a major fire in a Texas oil and petrochemicals complex has coincided with a leap in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Prices have risen in the markets for crude oil, and to an even greater degree, in those for gasoline as a response to the likely disruption of refining activity. This has not prevented the yen from bursting through the 105 level against the US dollar, which had for long been seen as the ceiling for the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Optimism over Japan's economic prospects ahead of tomorrow's release of the quarterly Tankan survey is the chief factor behind the yen's strength, as market participants mull their trading strategies for the new financial year. The key question that the yen's performance raises relates to the Japanese authorities' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy on the foreign exchanges. There were unconfirmed reports that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BoJ) did \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy dollars as the yen strengthened beyond 105 but that the scale of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was smaller than on past occasions.     Mr Tanigaki has since stated that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy remains unchanged. But he characterised the policy as one of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen's rise is deemed to be speculative while leaving the yen rate to market forces if the outcome reflects fundamentals. Clearly, the most important factor determining the BoJ's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is whether the Japanese authorities believe the market exchange rate for the yen does reflect the underlying fundamentals of the economy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fact that Mr Tanigaki is drawing attention to this way of looking at things suggests that policymakers in Tokyo are thinking hard about the fundamentals and may be in process of changing their view. The faction inside the BoJ which has been increasingly reluctant to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold the yen at 105, or weaker, appears to have made progress.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The implications for US Treasuries, which have benefited hugely from the BoJ's investment of the dollars it has acquired in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , are substantially negative.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasuries have enough problems closer to home. Mr Bernanke's comments yesterday raised the stakes in the run-up to Friday's release of non-farm payrolls data. He pointed out that the Federal Reserve's mandate called for it to pursue price stability and full employment. It followed that if prices were stable and the labour market was weak, the Fed was obliged to show 'patience' in holding interest rates low. Only were there indications showing either that inflation was no longer low or that the labour market was tightening, would the Fed need to review its policy-position. The markets are likely to increase their focus on non-farm payrolls and on the 'core' PCE deflator in response to these remarks. This could make US bond prices even more volatile than seemed likely before Mr Bernanke spoke, should either of those data-series deliver a shock.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though Mr Bernanke was expressing what is probably the consensus view of Fed Board Governors, some of the regional Fed presidents on the FOMC are clearly unhappy with current policy-settings. Mr Guynn, of the Atlanta Fed, is increasingly strident in his censure of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's line. Yesterday, he stated, without prevarication, 'we need to get rates to a more neutral level. If you keep rates too low for too long it can contribute to excesses'. Mr Guynn seems to be one of those against whom Mr Kohn was arguing last week when the latter played down the risks of asset price bubbles.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Atlanta Fed president finds sympathisers in other districts, with Mr Poole of St Louis saying that the Fed must be pre-emptive in its approach to dealing with inflation. On his logic, it will already be too late for effective Fed action, if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  waits for 'core' PCE inflation to pick up before making its move on interest rates. Mr Kohn is due to speak on monetary policy tomorrow when he will probably give the Greenspan team's reply to these criticisms.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The heated discussion over policy within Fed circles could increase market anxiety over the long-term consequences of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ultra-accommodative stance. There were no worries over 1% interest rates when everyone seemed to believe that inflation was negligible and would remain negligible. But what if Mr Guynn is right?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr Poole yesterday went on to say that the Fed would try to avoid raising interest rates near the time of the US election but would be ready to act even then, should economic conditions require a response. This comment perhaps explains why the debate within the Fed has broken out into the open at this time. Fed officials would prefer to do nothing on rates later this year. That means that if they are to do anything at all, they must move sooner rather than later.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is unrealistic to suppose that Mr Greenspan would court the opprobrium of losing the election for Mr George W Bush when he is blamed in some quarters for having lost the 1992 election for Mr Bush's father. The Fed Chairman is accused of having failed, then, to turn a 'jobless recovery' into an economic upturn accompanied by jobs aplenty. He is now likely to dig in his heels against his critics on the FOMC.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. [ 03-31-04 0615ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1089  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040331e03v00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
