\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off ahead of ECB rate decision, US jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 30 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Tuesday as investors kept buying euros to cover short positions despite investor caution at taking large bets ahead of a wide array of economic data due out later this week.  \par \par \uc2 Investors showed reluctance to take large bets ahead of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting and Japan's "tankan" survey of business sentiment, both due on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Friday brings the keenly-awaited U.S. non-farm payroll figures, which are expected to show that U.S. companies are finally hiring aggressively. This could raise expectations for a quicker end to the Federal Reserve's low interest rate stance - a move that would bolster the dollar and dollar denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 "It has been a very quiet day. But we are going to get some big numbers at the end of the week. We have the ECB (rate decision) and the (US) non-farm payroll numbers. My belief is that that number is going to be better than what most people think," said Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Montreal.  \par \par \uc2 POLL DATA BOLSTERS EURO  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar weakened against the euro after a Reuters poll showed 58 of the 60 economists predicted no change when the ECB's Governing Council meets.  \par \par \uc2 "This is an indicator as to why the market is buying back some of its short euro positions," Busch added.  \par \par \uc2 By early afternoon in New York, the euro was up 0.3 percent against the dollar at $1.2191 having tumbled to this year's low of $1.2050 on Monday, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 "Some of the market expectations of the near-term (ECB) rate cut have been washed out a little bit," said Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-AMRO Bank.  \par \par \uc2 US DATA  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect U.S. non-farm payrolls on Friday to have risen by around 103,000 in March, compared to 21,000 in February, with the market looking for clues as to when U.S. interest rates might start to rise from 1.0 percent - concerns that were heightened by a group of Federal Reserve officials speaking on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke said the reason for ailing job markets was big business productivity gains, but he noted that recent productivity growth is unsustainable. At the same time, he said it was hard to gauge when these productivity gains would slow.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar shed some of its losses after U.S. consumer confidence data eroded further in March after falling harder than expected in February as concerns over the labor market, rising gasoline prices and security concerns weigh on consumers.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board's US consumer confidence index fell to 88.3 in March from an upwardly revised 88.5 in February. Economists expected the index to fall to 86.5 in March.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN AND EUROPE  \par \par \uc2 Japan's closely watched tankan business survey on Thursday is expected to show further improvement in business sentiment particularly among big manufacturers benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand. An upbeat survey would suggest the economy can withstand a stronger exchange rate, putting even more question marks over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.1 percent at 105.64 yen , helped by position-adjustment ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end. The U.S. dollar slipped to a six-week low around 105.23 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.4 percent against the yen on the day at 128.80 after hitting a four-month low of 127.21 yen in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone data released earlier on Tuesday continued to support the less robust recovery that several European officials have warned about.  \par \par \uc2 Germany's BDI industry federation head Michael Rogowski said he had growing doubts about the pace of recovery in the euro zone's largest economy and called on the ECB to cut rates. Euro zone interest rates are currently 2.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03u000lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
