\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar dips ahead of ECB rate decision.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, March 30 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened broadly on Tuesday as investors bought euros to cover short positions despite caution at taking large bets ahead of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting and several key economic reports.  \par \par \uc2 Investors showed reluctance to shift positions radically ahead of an ECB meeting some had speculated could end with a reduction in interest rates. The general consensus, however, is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave rates steady on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "While most people do not expect the ECB to lower interest rates, I don't think anyone is fully confident that the possibility can be ruled out," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington D.C. "The general tone of the market is one of range trading."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders also were alert to economic data later this week from Japan and the United States.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Japan releases its "tankan" survey of business sentiment. Friday brings March U.S. non-farm payroll figures, which are expected to show that companies are finally hiring aggressively. This could raise expectations for a quicker end to the Federal Reserve's low interest rate stance - a move that would bolster the dollar and dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, traders will look to U.S. data on manufacturing in the Midwestern and New York regions. Another key report features data on U.S. factory orders.  \par \par \uc2 POLL DATA BOLSTERS EURO  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar weakened against the euro after a Reuters poll showed 58 of 60 economists surveyed predicted no change when the ECB's Governing Council meets.  \par \par \uc2 In late New York trade, the euro was up 0.12 percent against the dollar at $1.2167.  \par \par \uc2 "Some of the market's expectations for a near-term (ECB) rate cut have been washed out a little bit," said Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-AMRO Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.14 percent at 105.68 yen, helped by position-adjustment ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end. The U.S. dollar slipped to a six-week low around 105.23 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.30 percent against the yen on the day to 128.58 after hitting a four-month low of 127.21 yen in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. DATA  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect U.S. non-farm payrolls on Friday to have risen by around 103,000 in March, compared with 21,000 in February, with the market looking for clues as to when U.S. interest rates might start to rise from 1.0 percent - concerns that were heightened by a group of Federal Reserve officials speaking on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke said the reason for ailing job markets was big business productivity gains, but he noted that recent productivity growth is unsustainable. At the same time, he said it was hard to gauge when these productivity gains would slow.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar shed some of its losses after U.S. consumer confidence data eroded slightly for March after falling harder than expected in February as concerns over the labor market, rising gasoline prices and security weighed on consumers.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board's U.S. consumer confidence index fell to 88.3 in March from an upwardly revised 88.5 in February. Economists expected the index to fall to 86.5 in March.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN AND EUROPE  \par \par \uc2 Japan's closely watched tankan business survey on Thursday is expected to show further improvement in business sentiment, particularly among big manufacturers benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand. An upbeat survey would suggest the economy can withstand a stronger exchange rate, putting even more question marks over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u0036u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
