\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar still down after US consumer report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment; changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 30 (Reuters) - The dollar retraced some of its earlier losses on Tuesday following a positive U.S. consumer confidence report but investors kept buying euros to cover positions ahead of a Euro zone decision on interest rates later in the week.  \par \par \uc2 Investors, however, were reluctant to make large bets ahead of big events this week including a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting and Japan's "tankan" survey of business sentiment, both on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Friday brings the keenly-awaited U.S. non-farm payroll figures, which are expected to show that U.S. companies are finally hiring aggressively, which could raise expectations for a quicker end to the Federal Reserve's low interest rate stance.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro is just by default rising a little bit but there is no key direction. There is a bit of a correction, and some of the market expectations of the near-term (ECB) rate cut have been washed out a little bit," said Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-AMRO Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar shed some of its earlier losses on Tuesday after U.S. consumer confidence data eroded further in March after falling harder than expected in February as concerns over the labor market, rising gasoline prices and security concerns weigh on consumers.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board's US consumer confidence index fell to 88.3 in March from an upwardly revised 88.5 in February. Economists expected the index to fall to 86.5 in March.  \par \par \uc2 By mid morning in New York, the euro was up around 0.2 percent against the dollar at $1.2174 having tumbled to this year's low of $1.2050 on Monday, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro rose 0.3 percent on the day at 128.60 after hitting a four-month low of 127.21 yen in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.1 percent at 105.66 yen , helped by position-adjustment ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end. The U.S. dollar slipped to a six-week low around 105.23 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's tankan business survey on Thursday is expected to show further improvement in business sentiment particularly among big manufacturers benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand. An upbeat survey would suggest the economy can withstand a stronger exchange rate, putting even more question marks over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 ECB RATE DECISION AWAITED  \par \par \uc2 Recent comments by ECB officials increased expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be preparing the ground for a rate cut as soon as this week, taking the euro down to this year's low on Monday, but some say the selling might have been overdone.  \par \par \uc2 However, economists see a 25 percent chance the ECB will opt to cut interest rates when it meets on Thursday, according to a Reuters poll.  \par \par \uc2 Eurozone data released earlier on Tuesday continued to support the less robust recovery that several European officials have warned about.  \par \par \uc2 Germany's BDI industry federation head Michael Rogowski said he had growing doubts about the pace of recovery in the euro zone's largest economy and called on the ECB to cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone interest rates are currently 2.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. non-farm payrolls on Friday are expected to have risen by around 103,000 in March, compared to 21,000 in February, with the market looking for clues as to when U.S. interest rates might start to rise from 1.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u002fx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
