\par\par \b \uc2 US Treasuries hurting from jobs optimism.  \b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 30 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasuries stayed near their overnight lows and looked vulnerable on Tuesday as markets raised their expectations for U.S. jobs data due later this week.   \par \par \uc2  The fear of a drop in bids for Treasuries from foreign central banks seemed to have taken a back-seat as markets got over Monday's fuss over a report in a British newspaper suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy may have ended.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) shrugged off that report and said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to curb the yen's rise.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the prospect of less dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) and other Asian central banks, and therefore lower investment in U.S. markets, had hurt Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2  Benchmark U.S. 10-year bonds  were yielding 3.88 percent on Tuesday morning, just a tick down from Monday's 3.89 percent. That yield has climbed from 3.70 percent a week ago.   \par \par \uc2  "The bond market has sold off in the last four to five trading sessions," said Robert Rennie, strategist with Westpac Bank, Sydney.   \par \par \uc2  "One of the reasons has been the apparent step-back by the MOF and BOJ in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. That has seen Treasuries give back a couple of points," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Another factor was speculation about a strong rise in non-farm payrolls, which would hurt bond prices.   \par \par \uc2  In a week rich with data in the United States, the payroll numbers on Friday are eagerly awaited. If the data shows companies are finally hiring aggressively, as markets expect, it would raise expectations for a quicker end to the Federal Reserve's low-rates stance and cause bonds to be sold.   \par \par \uc2  TIME TO SELL TREASURIES?   \par \par \uc2  Ian Douglas, a U.S.-based strategist with UBS, said in a note that this week was crucial as it could potentially reverse the long-bullish trend in Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. inflation was bottoming out and levels of economic activity were rising, he said. If employment picked up too, monetary policy risks would rise.   \par \par \uc2  UBS has forecast 160,000 jobs being created in March, while a Reuters poll forecast payrolls expanding by 103,000 in March after just 21,000 in February.   \par \par \uc2  "It should also be a big enough gain to shock the bond market out of its complacent Fed view, albeit that more data will be needed before the Fed seriously contemplate tightening," Douglas's note said.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, data last week showed a drop in the holdings of U.S. Treasuries and debt by foreign central banks. Most of Asia's over $2 trillion in foreign reserves is invested in U.S. debt.   \par \par \uc2  Two-year notes  were yielding 1.59/1.58 percent on Tuesday, down from the previous day's 1.61 percent but higher than 1.47 percent a week ago.   \par \par \uc2  Westpac Bank's Rennie and other analysts said they would recommend that investors stay on the sidelines until the jobs report was released.   \par \par \uc2  But analysts at JP Morgan said they were bearish on bonds over the medium term.   \par \par \uc2  They would in particular sell the two-year Treasury note, which had become too expensive due to heavy bids from central banks and hedge funds using that segment for carry trades, Simon Klassen, JP Morgan's Singapore-based strategist said.   \par \par \uc2  The curve was too flat between one and two years and could steepen, while the spread between two and 30-year Treasuries would shrink, he said.   \par \par \uc2  The spread between two-and 30-year Treasuries was about 320 basis points.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u000ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
