\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks edge up, yen near 3-= yr high.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Marie Roantree  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 30 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near a 3-1/2-year low against the yen on Tuesday and Asian stocks edged up, buoyed by a Wall Street rise as investors recovered from security fears and looked with optimism to quarterly earnings.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices dipped on doubts OPEC will cut output at a meeting on Wednesday as planned, while the weak dollar pushed gold higher. Japanese bond prices rose as the market embraced slightly weaker-than-expected industrial output and employment data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was around 105.48 yen, flat from late U.S. levels and not far from its 3-= year low of 105.16 yen hit last month.  \par \par \uc2 Most stock indices in Asia rose, but gains in Tokyo were pared as investors took profits on recent high-flyers and amid caution the dollar could fall below 105 yen if Japanese corporate sentiment data on Thursday is better than expected.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei average had risen 0.3 percent by midday to 11,749.19, lifting the index up more than 10 percent so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 "If the dollar decisively breaks below 105 yen, that would stoke earnings fears and keep investors from buying high-tech issues," said Tetsuya Ishijima, senior investment strategist at Okasan Securities.  \par \par \uc2 An MSCI index of Asia Pacific shares outside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up 0.5 percent at 0240 GMT.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WORRIES  \par \par \uc2 Wariness about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from disappearing despite speculation Tokyo may scale back its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, keeping the yen in check for now.  \par \par \uc2 "They may not try to boost the dollar but there will still be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Etsuko Yamashita, chief economist at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp (SMBC).  \par \par \uc2 The euro, undermined recently by speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may cut rates to shore up the euro-zone economy, rose as some traders bought back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of an ECB policy meeting on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 It was quoted at $1.2173, up from around $1.2155 in late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose on Monday after volatile trading in the past three weeks dominated by global security fears after the deadly bombings in Madrid and Israel's killing of Hamas's spiritual leader.  \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average rose 1.1 percent to 10,329 and the technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index climbed 1.7 percent to 1,992. They both ended at their highest levels in more than two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The focus this week is on the ECB meeting to see if it cuts interest rates from two percent and U.S. jobs data, due on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in Hong Kong rose 0.7 percent, lifted by a 2.3 percent rise in conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa Ltd after it revealed plans to spin off its emerging markets telecoms business prior to a Hong Kong listing.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, exporters such as Toyota Motor Co, Honda Motor and Sony Corp all rose more than one percent despite the yen's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan shares were flat, held in check by investors taking profits after a strong rebound in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds rose, pushing the yield on the benchmark 258th 10-year bond down 1.5 basis points to 1.475 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese industrial output fell 3.7 percent in February, slightly more than forecasts for a 3.4 percent drop. February's jobless rate was unchanged at five percent, marginally higher than forecasts for a 4.9 percent rate.  \par \par \uc2 Gold edged up to $417.60/$418.25 an ounce compared with $416.50/$417.25 last quoted in New York, while U.S. crude oil fell 19 cents to $35.26 per barrel.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u000jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
