\par\par \b \uc2 Yen near recent peak on optimism over economy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 03:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 30 (Reuters) - The yen was steady on Tuesday near its recent three-and-a-half-year high against the dollar, supported by expectations of a solid recovery in the Japanese economy.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that Tokyo may scale back its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - rekindled by a British newspaper report on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would no longer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in the market - also supported the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Even so, wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from disappearing, keeping the yen in check for now despite another rally in Japanese shares.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0030 GMT, the dollar was around 105.50 yen flat from late U.S. levels and not far from its low of 105.16 yen hit last month.  \par \par \uc2 The market showed little immediate response to Japanese industrial output data.  \par \par \uc2 Production fell 3.7 percent in February from January, in line with expectations, after a sharp rise in January.  \par \par \uc2 The outlook remains positive, with manufacturers' output - a key component and close proxy of industrial production - expected by the government to rise 0.5 percent in March and 4.2 percent in April.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's benchmark Nikkei share average was up 0.5 percent in midmorning trade, keeping expectations alive that foreign capital will continue to flow into long-neglected Japanese shares.  \par \par \uc2 These factors led yen bulls to think it was only a matter of time before the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke through 105 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But question remained on what the Japanese authorities intended to do about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in February to stop the dollar from dropping below 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "They may not try to boost the dollar but there will still be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Etsuko Yamashita, chief economist at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp (SMBC). "I don't think the dollar will fall below 105 yen."  \par \par \uc2 The euro, undermined recently by speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) may cut rates to shore up the euro-zone economy, ticked up as some traders bought back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of an ECB policy meeting on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 It was quoted at $1.2165 from around $1.2155 in late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 "It's true that some economic indicators in the euro zone have been weak recently. But I think the ECB is still cautious about a rate cut," said SMBC's Yamashita  \par \par \uc2 "I don't expect a cut this week. The euro may rise a little after the meeting if the ECB does not ease," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, the market will be watching the U.S. consumer confidence index compiled by the Conference Board due at 1500 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters on average expect the index to fall to 86.5 in March from 87.3 in February, hurt by rising gasoline prices and worries about attacks after the Madrid bombings plus disappointment about a dearth of new jobs.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03u0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
