\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW: BOJ Tankan To Show Econ Recovery Broadening  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Asako Tanabe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan's closely watched March tankan survey due Thursday will show Japan's economic recovery is gathering pace and broadening to previously weak non-manufacturing sectors, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Given surging exports and production plus a recent pickup in personal consumption, economists surveyed by Dow Jones and Nikkei News expect on average that the tankan's headline index of sentiment among big manufacturers will improve to 10, up from seven in the revised December survey.  \par \par \uc2 Perhaps more significantly, economists on average expect the BOJ's index measuring sentiment among large non-manufacturers will turn positive for the first time since November 1996, rising four points to a reading of four.  \par \par \uc2 Large manufacturers themselves predicted in December that their sentiment index would edge slightly lower to six in March while non-manufacturers expected a rise to four.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ calculates the diffusion indexes by subtracting the percentage of respondents saying business conditions are bad from those saying they are good.  \par \par \uc2 All of the economists surveyed forecast that the diffusion indexes for both large manufacturers and non-manufacturers will post positive readings for the first time since November 1996.  \par \par \uc2 "The financial markets are already starting to factor in (positive tankan results) and expectations will rise for a further recovery of the economy," said Shuji Shirota, economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \par \par \uc2 The tankan survey results will be released at 2350 GMT Wednesday, which is 0850 Thursday morning in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      BOJ Revises Survey Methodology \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aside from expecting more upbeat sentiment, analysts will also examine the results closely to assess the impact of the BOJ's revision to its survey methodology.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many say the survey changes, published in early March, diminish the gap between sentiment among large manufacturers and non-manufacturers, partly due to changes in the way companies are grouped in the survey.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Previously, companies were grouped according to the number of employees, but the new method ranks firms according to their capital. A company is now considered large if it has Y1 billion or more in capital. Previously, companies were considered large if they had more than 1,000 employees.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The changes means that firms in newer and growing industries, such as internet-related enterprises, have been added to the non-manufacturer pool, and these companies will likely contribute to an improvement in the sentiment index, said Ryutaro Kono, chief economist at BNP Paribas in Tokyo.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the pace of improvement in the non-manufacturing sector is likely to be slow given weak domestic demand amid lingering deflation, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tankan now covers 10,848 companies, compared with 9,433 at the last review of the survey in March 1999. The number of companies surveyed dropped to around 8,200 by the time last December's tankan was done, however, due to reorganizations and bankruptcies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the key points to look for in the March tankan is whether the sentiment gap continues to shrink between large manufacturers and non-manufacturers and if the large manufacturers foresee further improvements in the coming three months, said Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein's Shirota.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The gap has already shrunk and I think that trend will become clearer," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But among smaller firms, the gap between manufacturers and non-manufacturers may widen further as manufacturers enjoy buoyant demand from rising exports while non-manufacturers remain sluggish due to a slower recovery in domestic demand, said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. The sentiment between large and smaller companies will also remain polarized, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Capex Likely To Start Low But Still Positive \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists also expect the survey will point to a continued strong rise in business investment next fiscal year, which starts Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On average, economists estimate large Japanese companies will forecast a 0.5% increase in business investment next fiscal year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That may seem a small rise compared with a forecast 3.5% rise in investment this fiscal year in December's revised tankan survey, but analysts say such a rise in the March survey would be positive for this time of year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Companies tend to be conservative in estimating investment early in the fiscal year and then revise up their plans as the year progresses.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A positive figure in a March survey would mean the trend is for a strong increase, Shirota said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In fiscal 2003, capital investment plans started out with a minus 0.8% average forecast, ending at up 5.2% (before the revision) in December, and this coming year will likely hold a similar pattern," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shirota expects large companies will on average forecast flat investment for fiscal 2004 in the March survey.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial market participants will also watch for firms' forecasts of the dollar-yen exchange rate to see if companies expect to operate with a higher yen in coming months given the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's continued strong appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the revised December survey, large manufacturers predicted an average dollar-yen rate in the second half of the current fiscal year from October to March of Y111.70, although the dollar has since traded as low as Y105.16 - a three-year low - on Feb. 11.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite a newspaper report Monday that quoted BOJ officials as saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's mammoth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign to stem the yen's rise is over, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have said they haven't altered their policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the yen's rise isn't in line with economic fundamentals.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forecasts of economists in the survey are as follows:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     Large Co. Sentiment  FY04 Capex Plans \par                             Mfg Non-Mfg    Large Cos \par    BNP Paribas              +12   +4       -1.6% \par    JCER                     +12   +1       -0.5% \par    S'tomo Mitsui Asset Mgmt +11   +5        0.4% \par    Dresdner KW              +11   +4        0.0% \par    UBS                      +11   +4 \par    UFJ Institute            +11   +4       -1.3% \par    HSBC Securities          +10   +5        3.0% \par    Goldman Sachs            +10   +4        1.0% \par    Merrill Lynch Japan      +10   +3 \par    Nikko Citigroup          +10   +3        1.0% \par    Nomura Research          +10   +3       -1.6% \par    Sumitomo-Life Research   +10   +3        1.5% \par    J.P. Morgan Securities   +10   +2        0.0% \par    Morgan Stanley Japan     +10   +2       -3.8% \par    Barclays Capital          +9   +5        5.0% \par    Shinkin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       +9