\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Quality Of Flows As Key To Japan As Quantity  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,059 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--For Japanese monetary authorities fighting the yen's appreciation, the quality of capital flows into the country seems to matter just as much as the quantity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 This can be seen by the shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics in recent weeks by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though the upward pressure on the yen remains strong, market participants say.  \par \par \uc2 Against a growing belief that Japan's economic recovery is for real this time, after so many false dawns in the last 14 years, foreign demand for Japanese securities - and the yen - is rampant. Yet \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been dramatically scaled back.  \par \par \uc2 That's partly because interest from the speculative trading community in pushing the yen higher has diminished greatly in the last few weeks after these investors, who typically take a shorter-term view of the market, got their fingers badly burned.  \par \par \uc2 Lulling these speculators into what proved to be a false sense of security, Japanese monetary authorites helped drive the dollar up as high as Y112.30 earlier this month before quickly withdrawing much of that support and letting the dollar tumble; it's now around 6% off from that peak. As evident in the latest data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago futures exchange, a good guide to the speculators' portfolios, a lot of these positions have been flushed out.  \par \par \uc2 This suggests Japan's finance ministry is more comfortable with the yen strengthening as a result of the world's second-largest economy attracting longer-term capital than short-term "hot" money inflows, market participants say.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's less concerned by real money than spec money," said a yen trader at a large U.S. investment bank in New York. "And as long as real money continues to flow into Japan you're going to see constant demand for yen."  \par \par \uc2 "Real" money refers to flows from longer-term investors, such as pension funds. It's considered a better guide to underlying investor sentiment toward a country and its economy than shorter-term hot money flows.  \par \par \uc2 Media reports in recent weeks suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is over. The latest, in the Times of London Monday, cited unnamed Bank of Japan officials as saying Japan's recovery is strong enough to withstand further yen gains, that Tokyo has decided there is no need to weaken the yen artificially and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when there's extraordinary volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, deny this, however. (The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the finance ministry.)  \par \par \uc2 "I think it is appropriate to say this article is based on speculation," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Tuesday. "There is no change to our basic stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move stably in reflection of fundamentals. If movements don't reflect fundamentals, we will take appropriate action."  \par \par \uc2 Market participants will know the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March when the ministry releases data for the month on Wednesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at ABN Amro cite a Nikkei News report that suggests the ministry spent around Y3-Y4 trillion in the month to keep the yen down. This would follow the Y10 trillion spent in the first two months of the year and last year's record Y20 trillion. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at protecting the country's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Capital Flowing Into Japan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there's still some guessing about where exactly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is taking its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy, what's in less doubt is foreign investor interest to buy Japanese stocks. On Tuesday, the benchmark Nikkei 225 index of Japanese shares traded as high as 11,869 points, its highest level since June 2002.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last week, foreign investors were net buyers of Japanese stocks for the sixth straight week as they bought into Japan's improving economic outlook. Foreign investors bought a net Y373.92 billion of Japanese shares in the week ended March 19, up from Y63.68 billion the previous week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And in February, there was a net Y717.8 billion inflow into Japan, according to the preliminary finance ministry data on the flow of securities investment into and out of Japan in February on a contract basis.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not quite the flow it was (a few weeks ago) but it does seem to be coming from abroad, especially the U.S.," said Neil Jones, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at Nomura International in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Arnim Holzer, senior investment strategist for asset allocation at Deutsche Asset Management in New York, says the funds he helps oversee specifically, worth around $600 million of the group's entire $750 billion portfolio, are overweight Japan about 2% above benchmark.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the near future, we think that's appropriate...and we still think Japanese equities merit attention," Holzer said. Noting the yen's bias remains skewed to the upside, he said this "means better returns for the equity holder."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday, the dollar touched a six-week low against the yen of Y105.30. Another 10 sen or so lower and it would have been trading at depths not seen since September 2000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This slide in the dollar is partly a result of these investment flows into Japan but also stems from speculators unwinding their massive long yen positions. The latest IMM data now show noncommercial accounts on the CME holding a net yen short position worth $1.7 billion. On March 9, these accounts held a combined net long dollar position worth $3.9 billion, one of the largest positions in three years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources at Japanese banks say they regularly get calls from MOF officials asking them about the size of speculative positions, where pre-placed stop-loss orders lie, the weight of positions on their order books and in particular how IMM accounts are positioned. Traders believe this is further evidence of Japan's bid to wipe the speculative influence from the yen's exchange rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 