\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls on report  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  ends  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comments; changes byline, dateline previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday after a newspaper report said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ended its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move that bolstered the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials denied the Times of London report, which was based on a Bank of Japan (BOJ) source, that they would no longer seek to weaken the yen and said their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 The report initially sent the yen to six-week highs against the dollar before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) reminded markets that it, and not the BOJ, controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and that it would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar weakness may be the theme today, everyone is focused right now on the story that came out on the Times about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co.  \par \par \uc2 "There is some room for modest euro rally but the trigger would be the yen," Rhame said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell against the euro after touching the highest level this year earlier on the global day as traders bet the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut interest rates on Thursday to support the bloc's fragile economic recovery. However, speculators reversed the trend and bought back the eurozone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The market is very disappointed that there was no follow-through on the break of $1.2050 level," said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "There are also some doubts creeping into the market about whether the ECB is going to really cut (rates) this Thursday or maybe wait until May. What you are seeing is people take some bets off the table," Walsh said.  \par \par \uc2 By early morning in New York, the dollar traded down about 0.4 percent at 105.48 yen , after hitting a six-week low. A break beyond 105.16 yen would be the yen's strongest in 3-1/2 years.  \par \par \uc2 The euro traded up 0.2 percent against the dollar at $1.2155 , after retracing earlier losses to $1.2050 - its lowest level so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell 0.3 percent against the yen at 128.17 yen after softening to 127.26 yen, a five-month low.  \par \par \uc2 UNCERTAIN TIMES  \par \par \uc2 The market will focus on the ECB's Thursday meeting to see if it cuts interest rates from its current 2 percent, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders believe the bank will not wait until its June meeting after President Jean-Claude Trichet said last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reassess its outlook if consumer demand does not improve.  \par \par \uc2 Other ECB officials reprised this dovish tune later in the week, with Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell saying on Friday Europe's recovery was uncertain and data mixed, with no sign that consumer demand was firming.  \par \par \uc2 Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders said on Monday it was up to the ECB to decide when the time was right to change interest rates, but he had noted many recent statements were pointing in that direction.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Times report, which quoted BOJ officials but not the Finance Ministry which directs \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that Japanese officials think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary because the country's economic recovery is gaining strength.  \par \par \uc2 A BOJ spokesman said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not in a position to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. But Finance Ministry officials reiterated that Tokyo would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a speculative article. There is no change in our policy," said Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has sold more than 30 trillion yen ($285 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2003 to curb the yen's strength, which could harm Japanese exporters.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040330e03t00142\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
