\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar weakens on word BOJ stops  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, March 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell steeply against the yen on Monday after a newspaper report said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ceased yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials denied the report, in the Times of London, which attributed the information to a Bank of Japan source. The source was reported as saying the BOJ would no longer seek to weaken the yen.   \par \par \uc2  The report initially sent the yen to six-week highs against the dollar before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reminded markets that it, and not the BOJ, controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and that it would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed.   \par \par \uc2  "That's definitely gaining the headlines," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "We're ready for slow and steady gains by the yen."   \par \par \uc2  The euro also regained some ground against the dollar, after the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  touched its lowest level this year earlier in the global day as traders bet the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut interest rates on Thursday to support the bloc's fragile economic recovery. However, speculators reversed the trend and bought back the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "The market is very disappointed that there was no follow-through on the break of $1.2050 level," said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank in New York.   \par \par \uc2  "There are also some doubts creeping into the market about whether the ECB is going to really cut (rates) this Thursday or maybe wait until May. What you are seeing is people taking some bets off the table," Walsh said.   \par \par \uc2  In late afternoon New York trading, the dollar was down 0.35 percent at 105.52 yen , after hitting a six-week low. A break beyond 105.16 yen would be the yen's strongest point in 3-1/2 years.   \par \par \uc2  The euro traded 0.23 percent higher at $1.2157 , recovering from $1.2050 - its lowest level so far this year.   \par \par \uc2  The euro fell 0.19 percent against the yen to 128.31 yen  after softening to 127.26 yen, a five-month low.   \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, markets may trade off a U.S. consumer confidence report that is expected to show confidence declining in March.   \par \par \uc2  UNCERTAIN TIMES   \par \par \uc2  The market will focus on the ECB's Thursday meeting to see if it cuts interest rates from the current 2 percent and on Friday's U.S. March employment data to see if the U.S. economy is recovering, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2  "We are going to see some position-squaring by the end of the day as (the market) awaits some major events. One is the ECB meeting on Thursday, and two is the U.S. employment numbers on Friday," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group Ltd.   \par \par \uc2  Some traders believe the ECB will not wait until its June meeting to cut rates after President Jean-Claude Trichet said last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reassess its outlook if consumer demand does not improve.   \par \par \uc2  Other ECB officials reprised this dovish tune later in the week, with Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell saying on Friday that Europe's recovery was uncertain and data mixed, with no sign that consumer demand was firming.   \par \par \uc2  Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders said on Monday it was up to the ECB to decide when the time was right to change interest rates, but he had noted many recent statements were pointing in that direction.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Times report, which quoted BOJ officials but not the Finance Ministry which directs \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that Japanese officials think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary because the country's economic recovery is gaining strength.   \par \par \uc2  A BOJ spokesman said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not in a position to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. But Finance Ministry officials reiterated that Tokyo would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed.   \par \par \uc2  "It is a speculative article. There is no change in our policy," said Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki.   \par \par \uc2  Japan has sold more than 30 trillion yen ($285 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2003 to curb the yen's strength, which could harm Japanese exporters. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t002l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
