\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-ECB rate speculation hits euro, Wall St seen up.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nigel Stephenson  \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 15:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 29 (Reuters) - Speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be about to cut interest rates pushed the euro lower against the dollar on Monday and European shares rose before key U.S. economic data later this week.   \par \par \uc2  A newspaper report, denied by Japanese authorities, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's gains had officially ended pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.   \par \par \uc2  Wall Street looks likely to open higher and oil prices fell as traders awaited news of whether OPEC would implement planned production cuts.   \par \par \uc2  With growing doubts in investors' minds about the strength of economic recovery, many are speculating the ECB will cut the cost of borrowing at its meeting on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Institute of Supply Management issues its survey of the manufacturing sector on Thursday and, the following day, the U.S. Labor Department releases keenly-awaited jobs data for March. Surprisingly weak payrolls growth figures for February led markets to push back their expectations of higher U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  "This is a big week from a macro point of view and there will be a lot of uncertainty ahead of the ECB meeting on Thursday and the U.S. payrolls data on Friday," said Nigel Cobby, managing director of European equities at JPMorgan.   \par \par \uc2  European shares rose, fuelled by strong Chinese sales from Volkswagen and oil giant BP's plans to continue buying back shares.   \par \par \uc2  The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was 0.86 percent higher while the narrower DJ Euro STOXX 50 index ticked up 0.82 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Worries the ECB might cut rates briefly pushed the euro down to its lowest level this year against the dollar and a four-month low against the yen.   \par \par \uc2  "ECB risks this week are certainly weighing on it. If the ECB does not ease this week the you could see a rebound in euro/dollar," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London.   \par \par \uc2  A story in The Times of London, citing Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sources, said Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign had "officially" ended. This sent the yen to six week highs on the dollar before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) reminded markets it controls \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and would continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as needed.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was last at $1.2119, off a low of $1.2050. The dollar was at 105.79 yen.   \par \par \uc2  In government bond markets, the interest rate sensitive two year Schatz yield was at 2.1 percent, up 4.8 basis points as investors took profits on last week's big gains. The benchmark 10-year Bund yield was up 3.9 basis points at 3.94 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei share index ended lower after three sessions of gains as investors became nervous and took profits on builders and steel makers. The yen's rise against the dollar also weighed on exporters such as Sony.   \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei fell 0.45 percent and the broader TOPIX index closed up 0.24 percent.   \par \par \uc2  "Overall market sentiment is still firm but caution started emerging and short-term investors were seen starting to cash in profits," said Hiroyuki Nakai, chief strategist at Tokai Tokyo Securities.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock index futures were higher, pointing to a positive start on Wall Street later.   \par \par \uc2  Brent crude oil for May was down 40 cents a barrel at $31.59 on as traders awaited developments in Vienna where OPEC ministers are meeting on Wednesday to decide whether to go ahead with a planned one million barrels a day output cut from April 1.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t00159\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
