\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Markets read yen strength in mixed Japan rhetoric.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 29 (Reuters) - The Japanese rhetoric may be conflicting but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are all set to buy the yen anyway on the belief that a strengthening economy and rising stock market will eventually boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) on Monday dismissed a report in a British newspaper, the Times, that quoted a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  official as saying its yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ended.  \par \par \uc2 To some in the market, the episode was a reminder that the BOJ, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen markets, and the MOF, which decides \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, did not see eye-to-eye.  \par \par \uc2 Others thought it was a carefully plotted plan by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and MOF to progressively communicate a softening in the government's weak-yen policy preference.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose to within striking distance of last month's highs near 105 per dollar on Monday, before markets got nervous about testing the Bank of Japan's resolve.  \par \par \uc2 "The MOF makes the call, the BOJ is merely the agent," said Patrick Bennett, a Singapore-based strategist with Commerzbank.  \par \par \uc2 "But, regardless of the comments, there is merit in the conclusion that if the economy performs strongly and the equity market is on an upward trajectory then the safety to stand against yen strength in the same fashion as recently has been diminished.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is going to continue to buy the yen and they are buying yen because of a strong economy," Bennett said.  \par \par \uc2 While many in the market remained sceptical that Japan's economy could cope with a sharply higher yen, the Times quoted a BOJ official as saying the authorities now could be confident that the recovery no longer depended on export strength.  \par \par \uc2 All the market needed was a trigger, analysts said, to push the yen beyond 105 per dollar, and that could come from this week's quarterly "tankan" business survey or the start of the new fiscal year in April.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei stock average hit a 22-month high on Monday. Economic growth rose to an annual 6.4 percent, the fastest in 13 years, in October-December. Last week, rating agency Standard & Poor's upgraded its outlook on Japan to stable.  \par \par \uc2 "Irrespective of the MOF, irrespective of the Times, the driver could be the fundamentals that continue to improve," said Claudio Piron, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Standard Chartered Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "That really does leave 105 as a formality."  \par \par \uc2 PROOF IN THE PUDDING  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell six percent between February and early March, which markets attributed partly to fiscal year-end window-dressing.  \par \par \uc2 But it has reversed all those losses in the past three weeks, despite the dollar's relative firmness against the commodity and European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That, analysts said, was proof that the authorities were preparing to loosen their grip on markets.  \par \par \uc2 "What is probable is that the BOJ may be reflecting what is MOF policy, so we must take them quite seriously," said Jake Moore, a strategist with Barclays Capital in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "What is determining all this is action and it seems as if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slowed," Moore said.  \par \par \uc2 And one could hardly expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to openly acknowledge a policy shift, causing runaway gains in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has not changed and it is that of preventing excessive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But their tactics for dealing with dollar/yen have changed," Moore said.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF statement on Monday made that clear. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would be aimed at preventing excessive volatility, it said, and traders suspected there was some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day.  \par \par \uc2 Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo, said fund managers were buying the yen on Monday but corporations were waiting for the year-end. Plus, a lot of Japanese dollar claims had already been hedged during the yen's steep drop to near 112.3 early this month, he said.  \par \par \uc2 CRITICISM GETS LOUDER  \par \par \uc2 The criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, which economists sometimes describe as one of the world's largest carry trades, is getting louder.  \par \par \uc2 The United States wants the undervalued yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to absorb some dollar weakness so that its massive current account deficit can be lowered.  \par \par \uc2 Even if Japan ignores external pressures, policymakers are being questioned about the wisdom of having spent 30 trillion yen ($285 billion) since the start of last year to prop up an export sector that contributes just over 10 percent of real GDP.  \par \par \uc2 Fiscal imprudence aside, the dollars are invested in low-yielding short-term U.S. Treasuries. Japan is running a notional loss on the exchange front, having bought dollars when they were expensive, and risks losses on the yields if Japanese rates start rising faster than in the United States. ($1=105.28 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t000v0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
