\par\par \b \uc2 Yen rises on speculation  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  done with  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 29 (Reuters) - The yen closed in on a multi-year peak versus the dollar on Monday following a newspaper report that Japan had ended its campaign to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the economy no longer needed propping up.  \par \par \uc2 Officials at both the Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan (BOJ) said that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed, playing down the report and beating back the yen from the day's highs.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the story on the Web site of the Times of London made dollar bulls nervous, hammering home the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot rely on massive \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forever and needed an exit strategy.  \par \par \uc2 "I was sceptical about the article at first," said Satoshi Tokuda, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp. "But then I started to think it might be true. They might not stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly, but they may scale it back gradually.  \par \par \uc2 The report, which quoted BOJ officials but not the Finance Ministry which directs \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that Japanese officials think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary because the country's economic recovery is gaining strength.  \par \par \uc2 But Finance Ministry officials reiterated on Monday that Tokyo would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a speculative article. There is no change in our policy," said Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki.  \par \par \uc2 A BOJ spokesman also said it could not confirm the Times report, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not in a position to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has sold more than 30 trillion yen ($285 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2003 to curb the yen's strength, which could harm Japanese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 At 0601 GMT, the dollar was around 105.60 yen, still down about 0.4 percent from around 106 yen in late New York on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped below 105.30 yen in early Tokyo trade, its lowest level since mid-February when it fell to a three-year low of 105.16 yen before rebounding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 FOCUS ON 105 YEN  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also surged against the euro to a 4-= month high of around 127.40 yen  \par \par \uc2 While many dealers assume that Japan will not suddenly quit its yen-selling campaign, they were also pondering how far Japan would tolerate the yen edging up as the nation's economic recovery continues.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the focus is on the key 105-yen area, seen as a major defence line for the Japanese government and the rate forecasted by many of Japan's major exporters such as auto firms for the second half of the current business year.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts think the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will break through 105 yen soon.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar will eventually fall below 105 yen. It's a matter of time," said Junya Tanase, strategist at JPMorgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 The euro eased about 0.3 percent versus the dollar to $1.2085 near a three-week low of $1.2070 hit on Friday, hurt by speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut rates soon,  \par \par \uc2 Last week the head of Germany's influential Ifo economic research institute urged a ECB rate cut to help support the euro zone's fragile recovery. ($1=105.28 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t000np\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
