\par\par \b \uc2 Yen hits 5-week high on report  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  over.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 29 (Reuters) - The yen hit a five-week peak against the dollar on Monday on a newspaper report that Japan had ended its campaign to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But the yen quickly backed off from highs as officials at both the Finance Ministry and the Bank of Japan (BOJ) sought to play down the report.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders also suspected the authorities had stepped in to the market to quell speculation about a policy shift.  \par \par \uc2 "Many still think the authorities will protect the 105 yen level for the year-end on March 31," said Yoshihiro Nomura, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Trust & Custody Services Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The yen jumped to 105.26 per dollar in early trade, before retreating to around 105.77 on what some traders said was dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen's surge was triggered by a report on the Web site of the Times of London that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had officially come to an end.  \par \par \uc2 The article, which quoted BOJ officials and not the Finance Ministry which directs \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that Japanese officials think that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no longer necessary because the country's economic recovery is gathering strength.  \par \par \uc2 But a senior official at the ministry reiterated on Monday that Tokyo would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed while declining to comment on whether it had ordered the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "We have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  all along based on the Boca Raton statement (by the Group of Seven) - that excessive volatility and disorderly movement are undesirable - and we will continue to do so if necessary," he told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 A BOJ spokesman also said it could not confirm the Times report, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not in a position to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has sold more than 30 trillion yen ($285 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early 2003 to curb the yen's strength, which could harm Japanese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 At 0315 GMT, the dollar was around 105.60 yen, still down about 0.4 percent from around 106 yen in late New York on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 FOCUS ON 105 YEN  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also surged against the euro to a 4-= month high of around 127.50 yen  \par \par \uc2 The euro eased about 0.3 percent versus the dollar to $1.2085 near a three-week low of $1.2070 hit on Friday, hurt by speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut rates soon.  \par \par \uc2 While many dealers assume that Japan will not suddenly curb its yen-selling campaign, they were also pondering how far Japan would tolerate the yen edging up as the nation's economic recovery continues.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the focus is on the key 105-yen area, seen as a major defence line for the Japanese government and the rate forecasted by many of Japan's major exporters such as auto firms for the second half of the current business year.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a three-year low of 105.16 yen in February before rebounding on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But some analysts think the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will break through 105 yen soon.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar will eventually fall below 105 yen. It's a matter of time," said Junya Tanase, strategist at JPMorgan Chase. ($1=105.28 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t000em\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
