\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF says will continue  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  policy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 109 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said on Monday it would continue its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , shrugging off a British newspaper report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign.   \par \par \uc2  "We have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  all along based on the Boca Raton statement (by Group of Seven countries) - that excessive volatility and disorderly movement are undesirable - and we will continue to do so if necessary," a senior MOF official told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be a lot of talk about a policy change in March or April, but there is no change in our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040329e03t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
