\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Harvard Academic:Higher CNY In China Interest  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Owen Brown  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 810 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 13:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Allowing the U.S. dollar to collapse isn't in China's long-term interest, Harvard Business School Associate Dean Richard Vietor said, as he urged Beijing to consider a gradual appreciation in the yuan exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Vietor said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he estimates to be undervalued by between 20% and 25%, should be allowed to gradually appreciate toward 7 yuan to the dollar "and pretty soon."  \par \par \uc2 The visiting academic admits it's an argument that is falling on deaf ears in Beijing, where the leadership has ruled out short-term changes to its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 But Vietor argues the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in China and Japan can only have one of two eventual outcomes - a collapse in the dollar or higher U.S. trade barriers.  \par \par \uc2 "It isn't in China's long-term interest for the U.S. to go broke, it isn't in Japan's long-term interest," Vietor told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview.  \par \par \uc2 Vietor's couching of the argument in terms of China's own self-interest comes amid increasing pressure from the U.S. for Beijing to relax the country's tight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow met for an hour Friday with Zhou Xiaochuan, the governor of the People's Bank of China, in Washington D.C., during which time he urged Beijing to begin preparations to phase out its fixed-exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 That pressure is expected to continue when a U.S. delegation headed by U.S. Commerce Department Undersecretary Grant Aldonas and Deputy Trade Representative Josette Sheran arrive in China Thursday for trade-related discussions.  \par \par \uc2 Vietor said he isn't advocating a sudden free-float of the yuan against the dollar but rather a gradual increase in the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as renminbi.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China could control it totally because its foreign reserves are so large," he said. "And companies can adjust to it trading-wise if it's done over three to four years."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    USD Collapse Possible Without China Help \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is only fully convertible on the trade account and even then the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has held the value at 8.3 yuan to the dollar for almost a decade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That fixed exchange rate weathered the fall in regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the Asian financial crisis but now it's the U.S. dollar that is in decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has fallen about 50% against the euro since its peak in July 2001.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That huge loss in ground by the dollar is putting both market and political pressure on China and other countries with similar \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls to raise their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Similarly, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prevent a sharp rise in the yen against the declining dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both of these measures prevent any adjustment in the U.S. trade deficit while Japan and China continue to build up U.S. dollar assets as their foreign reserves - already the two largest in the world - continue to grow.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. trade deficit with China expanded sharply to $11.48 billion in January from $9.87 billion in December. The deficit with Japan narrowed slightly to $5.25 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Vietor argues U.S trade shortfalls, compounded by a U.S. budget deficit that could possibly grow beyond an estimated $520 billion in 2003, will eventually lead to a collapse in the dollar, "certainly against the yen", unless Beijing and Tokyo help the U.S. fix its trade imbalance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The era of U.S. trade deficits is coming to an end soon, Vietor said. "And the easiest way to deal with it would be to let the renminbi appreciate gradually," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The alternative, given the U.S. presidential election in November this year, will be increased trade barriers against China, Vietor said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Not because you (China) are doing something wrong but because we haven't any other way to deal with it," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If (U.S. Democratic presidential hopeful) John Kerry gets elected, I can tell you what the solution is going to be, it will be trade barriers," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if (U.S. President) George Bush stays in office."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietor acknowledged arguments in Beijing that the U.S. should have to respond to its own economic problems rather than force a solution on China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he said China has to realize that, for whatever reasons, the U.S. is unlikely to address its own problems.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's our problem, but you (China) have to help us fix it," Vietor said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 College Web site:  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.hbs.edu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.hbs.edu}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Owen Brown, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; owen.brown@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Sharon Buan [ 29-03-04 1141GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040329e03t000mw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
