\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Hayashi: No Change In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A top Japanese Finance Ministry official reiterated Monday that Tokyo's policy of stemming excessive volatility in exchange rates is unchanged, countering speculation that Tokyo is about to end its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We have said that rapid (exchange rate) changes are undesirable and that we will take appropriate action against them. There is no change in our thinking," Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi told a regular press conference. [ 29-03-04 0842GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Hayashi was speaking after a Times of London report cited unnamed Bank of Japan sources as saying Tokyo has decided there is no more need to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 He added that he couldn't comment on current exchange rates, on whether they reflected fundamentals now, and on whether the government has left unchanged the huge scale of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Times report also cited the BOJ officials as indicating they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market only when there's extraordinary volatility. The sources "made clear that the unprecedented dollar purchases of the past seven months were formally over", the newspaper reported.  \par \par \uc2 But the BOJ acts only as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's agent in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it has no authority over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy under the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Law.  \par \par \uc2 Asked whether the ministry had spoken to the BOJ about the Times article, Hayashi said the MOF hadn't specifically discussed the "speculative media report" with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson [ 29-03-04 0901GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040329e03t000fu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
