\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Watanabe: No Change To MOF  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 256 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remains unchanged, a senior Japanese finance ministry official said Monday, although he declined to comment on a newspaper report saying that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign has come to an end.  \par \par \uc2 "I have absolutely nothing to say regarding the contents of the report," Hiroshi Watanabe told Dow Jones Newswires on article in the Times of London. "But with regard to the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy - it remains unchanged."  \par \par \uc2 The Times report quoted unnamed BOJ sources as saying Tokyo has decided that there is no more need to weaken the yen artificially. It also cites the BOJ officials as indicating they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market only when there's extraordinary volatility. The sources "made clear that the unprecedented dollar purchases of the past seven months were formally over", the newspaper reports.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ acts only as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's agent in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has no authority over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy under the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Law.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a low of Y105.29 - within striking distance of a three-year low of Y105.16 - as traders reacted to the report. But it later recovered amid speculation that it was unlikely the MOF would withdraw completely from the market, especially at current levels. As of 0026 