\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: JGBs Turn Virtuous Bond Cycle Vicious  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,099 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 00:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Brian Blackstone \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Higher Japanese government bond yields suggest the virtuous global fixed income cycle characterizing the start of 2004 could crumble in the wake of global economic recovery and rising price pressures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Japan's 10-year government note yield, which moves inversely to price, rose 8.5 basis points Monday to 1.485%, close to a four-month high, capping a 0.18 percentage point rise in the past three sessions. The 10-year Treasury yield has also risen 18 basis points to 3.90% during that time, while the German 10-year is up nine basis points to 3.95%.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's fixed-income market is a good leading indicator for global bonds, analysts reckon. Last year, Japanese bonds started selling off in May, a precursor to a global stampede out of government securities in late June. And last month, JGBs racked up nine-straight days of price gains and falling yields, similarly leading a global rally that brought U.S. and euro-zone benchmarks well below 4%.  \par \par \uc2 "Certainly since last year's slight rally and mini bond crash, there's been a high correlation" between JGBs and global bonds, said Mike McGlone, Treasurys trader at ABN AMRO Futures in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "As far as I'm concerned the (JGB) market's broken down," which could in turn push the 10-year Treasury towards 4.40%, he added, assuming the U.S. government reports moderate payroll growth for March on Friday. Estimates currently center around such growth, with jobs up 115,000.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the Japanese bond market is leading the way...but the payroll report is going to largely condition the U.S. fixed income market," said Larry Kantor, global head of market strategy at Barclays Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The selloff in Japan could have broader implications however, for a very popular global fixed income trade. With Bank of Japan short-term official rates at zero, Japan has encouraged borrowing at low rates and buying longer-dated, higher-yielding securities - the so-called "carry trade" - which has been replicated in the U.S., where the Federal Reserve's official rate is at a 46-year low of 1%.  \par \par \uc2 "A steep yield curve is encouraging of duration and leveraging of credit," said Sudi Mariappa, head of global portfolio management at PIMCO in Newport Beach, Calif., the world's largest bond fund with $350 billion under management. "Whenever you have deleveraging of that, you look to the Japanese because they have a lot of liquidity," he added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Durable Recovery \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One difference between now and 2003, analysts say, is the durable nature of Japan's recovery. It grew at a 6.4% pace in the fourth quarter and is expected to expand around 3% this year. Japan retail sales rose on a year-over-year basis in February, the second-straight gain, which analysts said was a sign its export recovery has seeped to the domestic economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy, the world's second largest, has largely been written off in the past decade. So despite some disappointment on global economic data, particularly from Europe, any sign that Japan is on the path to self-sustained recovery would bolster the global economy's prospects and weigh on fixed-income markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We feel comfortable in our bearish view on JGBs as it is supported by upside economic surprises and a rallying equity market," analysts at JPMorgan in London wrote in a research note. The firm sees upside potential to its forecast of 3.4% 2004 GDP growth there. "We advise a significant short duration position in JGBs," the analysts added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Japan grows domestically, it would also mean less need for Japanese authorities to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets to weaken the yen and strengthen the dollar. Proceeds from dollar buying are channeled into dollar-denominated assets, principally Treasurys. Because of that, massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the past year has helped keep U.S. market interest rates low, fueling a consumer spending-led expansion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now, a string of reports, the latest one Monday by the Times of London, have suggested the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will end large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , although \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have denied any such shift and stressed the BOJ doesn't set \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. The BOJ is simply the government's agent in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Euro-Zone Debt Should Outperform \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, JPMorgan recommends overweighting government debt in the euro area, Canada and, to a lesser extent, Australia versus the U.S., U.K. and Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Canada is thought to be in an easing cycle and many analysts think the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will slice rates soon. Though Australia raised rates by a half point since late last year, recent housing numbers suggest it can pause before doing so again. In contrast, the Bank of England is expected to continue hiking rates, while remarks from Fed officials may pave the way for higher rates later this year. And markets are now pricing in the chance of BOJ tightening by summer 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, many strategists have favored euro-zone debt since the start of the year - figuring that eventually a rising euro and weak economic growth would favor returns on European debt - and been disappointed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the euro hasn't risen as high as many had anticipated this year, expectations that the ECB may cut rates as early as Thursday in response to persistently weak demand helped bonds there withstand the latest global selloff, with German yields rising only half as much as in the U.S. and Japan. ECB officials have in recent days stressed weak growth and falling confidence, leading ECB watchers to raise expectations for a rate cut.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though "there'll be selling pressure everywhere" if the JGB slump continues, "the euro zone could buck the trend," ABN Amro's McGlone said, given its bond-friendly mix of weak growth, tame inflation and expectations of monetary easing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The five-year in Europe still represents value," said PIMCO's Mariappa, while "Australia and the U.K. are interesting because the (yield) curve's flattening" and inflation-adjusted yields are high, particularly in the U.K.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Brian Blackstone writes about U.S. and global markets for Dow Jones Newswires. Previously, he had covered the Federal Reserve and European economics and politics for Dow Jones in New York and Paris)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Brian Blackstone; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2137; brian.blackstone@dowjones.com [ 03-29-04 1709ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040329e03t00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi