\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Is  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Over?  \b0\par\par\uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 18:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Times of London report today that Japan's campaign to weaken the yen and strengthen the dollar has officially come to an end, based on an exclusive briefing by Bank of Japan officials in Tokyo, has caused quite a stir in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , bond and stock markets globally, not to mention within the Japanese government, pitting officials from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the unnamed BOJ officials who briefed the London newspaper.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But the appearance of conflict between MOF and the BOJ is more a tempest in a teapot, than anything serious. And financial markets may be overreacting to the Times article, which arguably states the obvious. Perhaps the main objection MOF has with the BOJ officials who briefed the Times is confirming what many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had already discerned ... the days of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive dlr/yen higher ended in early March. Buying up to $20 billion in dlr/yen on March 5 after a weak U.S. February jobs report will not be seen again. However, smoothing the move down in dlr/yen will continue. The article also concluded that some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be needed in periods of extreme volatility. However, the article seemed to fall back on a misleading 'sound bite' ... "The end of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". Indeed, it is widely assumed that MOF had the BOJ buy dollars near Y105.25 today.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ officials who spoke to the Times didn't forget who makes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy decisions in Japan ... the MOF, not the BOJ. And surely BOJ Governor Fukui has been very supportive of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, even when they seemingly went off the deep-end earlier this month. Indeed Fukui was made Governor by Prime Minister Koizumi because he would be supportive of MOF economic policies and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in particular. This was not the case before Fukui was made Governor. Governor Hayami was known as a strong yen advocate and often clashed with MOF policymakers over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. Moreover, Deputy Governor Mutoh was named to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Fukui because he came from MOF's International Bureau and was very familiar with MOF's weak yen policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 OK, one could argue that others besides Fukui and Mutoh at the BOJ are less enamored with the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  orientation ... and with reason. But this is not behind the Times article today. Instead, the Times report simply reflects the decision made by MOF earlier this month to shift \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics from massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at driving dlr/yen higher to smoothing the move down.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ officials who spoke with the Times were simply framing the economic reasons for the change in the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ... the recovery is on track, deflation is showing signs of abating, and the domestic economy is showing signs of life from business investment to consumer spending. As the article noted, the BOJ indicated that growth was no longer mainly export-dependent. It is highly unlikely that any BOJ official would proffer this point of view without the backing of the government or the MOF.  \par \par \uc2 Perhaps the best explanation for the BOJ briefing is the desire to give weight to notion that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy went from high burn to simmer for fundamental reasons, and not because of protests from U.S. officials (Snow and Greenspan). This may be more about saving face than leaking the obvious ... the days of $10 billion-to-$20 billion a day in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are over and we think will never be seen again by a single \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead we expect the MOF to order BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a move under Y105 through Wednesday, the end of Japan's fiscal year. And beyond Wednesday it is very likely that MOF allows the market to trade lower, toward Y100, albeit gradually with the BOJ under MOF orders smoothing the move down. Meanwhile, between the ECB moving toward a rate cut (we think May over April), and no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue for either the ECB or BOJ in euro/yen, there is considerable downside in this pair ... Y110 in due course, Y124 near-term. And the reduction in MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reduce the demand for U.S. Treasurys over time and, all else being equal, will pressure U.S. yields higher.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.) [ 03-29-04 1159ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040329e03t0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
