\par\par \b \uc2 MONUMENT SECURITIES: Threats To US Treasurys  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Lewis  \par \uc2 1,108 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 13:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Further evidence is emerging of a split among Japanese policymakers over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics on foreign exchanges.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF) protests there has been no change in exchange rate policy. But there is no reason to doubt The Times of London had a reliable Bank of Japan (BoJ) source for today's story that the phase of heavy Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is over.  \par \par \uc2 As we pointed out in our Briefing No 2021, there is a faction in the BoJ that is becoming restive about the heavy dollar purchases that the exchange rate policy has entailed. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the recent scale complicates money market management and, at least on paper, leaves the BoJ's own balance sheet heavily exposed to US market conditions.  \par \par \uc2 Mr Fukui, the BoJ Governor, appears to incline to this view. But Japan's exchange rate policy is the responsibility of the MoF, not of the BoJ. The BoJ can only try to persuade MoF officials to take on board its concerns. A BoJ media campaign, such as has been under way for the past two weeks, is one way of making the point.  \par \par \uc2 These are nervous days for holders of US Treasuries. Until there is full clarification of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official position on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they will not be able to gauge how much, if any, Treasuries purchases the BoJ will undertake in future.  \par \par \uc2 The performance of the Treasuries market last Friday showed its sensitivity to this question. Then, the sharpest one-day jump in the 10-year benchmark yield for two months occurred in the session that followed release of Federal Reserve data showing foreign central banks' holdings of US Treasury and agency bonds had fallen by $1.568bn in the week ended 24 March. That was the first reported weekly decline in these holdings for twenty-one weeks.  \par \par \uc2 It seemed to represent early confirmation that there had, indeed, been a shift in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and that BoJ, as a result, had no extra funds to invest in the US bond markets. The 10-year yield, having touched a low point for this year of 3.68%, when foreign central banks were still reckoned to be net buyers, closed Friday's trading at 3.84%.  \par \par \uc2 There have been varying estimates of the extent to which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying earlier in the year depressed Treasury yields. The consensus appears to be that the 10-year yield might have been 30-40 bps higher if the central banks had not been investing the dollar proceeds of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If there were to be no more support for the market from this source, Treasury yields would presumably adjust, with the 10-year yield heading back above 4.0%.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed data is not conclusive in proving that a shift in Japanese policy has occurred. The US dollar staged a rally against the yen in the period covered by the figures. The BoJ would not have had occasion to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the yen exchange rate even if it had been following much the same policy as in January and February.  \par \par \uc2 Since 24 March, however, the dollar has fallen against the yen and is back in the range where BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been especially heavy in the past. The US Treasury market will, therefore, be scanning the foreign exchanges anxiously to see whether the BoJ steps in to curb the yen's strength. If it does not, it will be a sign that Mr Fukui and his BoJ colleagues are winning the argument over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. A BoJ victory on this issue would carry negative implications for US Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 An end to foreign central banks' buying of US bonds is not the only threat to the Treasuries market. There is also the risk that recent market strength, stemming from widespread expectations the Fed will hold short-term interest rates at ultra-low levels indefinitely, will unwind in a disorderly manner.  \par \par \uc2 After all, to say the timing of the next Fed rate hike is indefinite is not to say a rate hike will never happen. Some Fed officials have gone to great lengths to underline this point. They are probably mindful of the threat to the US economy and financial markets that arose last summer when markets eventually decided that the risks of deflation had been greatly overstated. The Treasuries sell-off created a dynamic, through its impact on mortgage financing, that probably still exerts a dampening effect on household spending.  \par \par \uc2 We all know that the Fed's strategy is to exercise patience in judging when to pull the trigger on rates. In the meantime, Fed officials are able to choose in their public utterances whether to stress the patience or the fact that rates will eventually rise.  \par \par \uc2 The apparently well-orchestrated chorus of Fed officials emphasising the latter aspect of the situation last week is probably best seen as an attempt on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's part to bring bond yields back up to sustainable levels. It seems unlikely there has been any substantial shift in the Fed's position, however.  \par \par \uc2 The comment of Mr Kohn, a Fed Governor who is especially close to Mr Greenspan, was almost lost in the hubbub. He said: 'some observers argue that the Federal Reserve has already been too patient...But I think the hurdle is high - and appropriately so - for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten policy, and in the process damp an expansion of economic activity in the short run, on the suspicion that movements in asset prices and increases in debt threaten economic stability over the longer run.' This seems to be the authentic voice of the Greenspan Fed.  \par \par \uc2 -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com  \par \par \uc2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)  \par \par \uc2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. [ 03-29-04 0625ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1089  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040329e03t00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
