\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits five-week low against yen then steadies.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 26 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a five-week low against the yen on Friday after further positive news on the Japanese economy but steadied by midsession after suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It held steady against the euro, supported by speculation about a rise in U.S. interest rates after U.S. Federal Reserve officials warned on Thursday about the need for an increase in borrowing costs eventually.  \par \par \uc2 While noting the threat of a swelling U.S. budget deficit, St. Louis Fed Governor William Poole and Fed Board Governor Donald Kohn said on Thursday that a future interest rate rise was inevitable given recent indications of U.S. economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 The yen drove up to 105.63 per dollar , a level not seen since February 18, before easing back to the 106 level on what traders said was dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities around the 105.70 level.  \par \par \uc2 But its slide back to 106 did little to change market perceptions and some traders said it was just a matter of time before it broke through the 105 level, given its jump of nearly six percent from the 112 level in just three weeks.  \par \par \uc2 "Just a month ago, there was concern about the yen's outlook, but looking at its position right now, you have to consider what's changed, and that's this piling up of positive fundamental data," said a manager at a Japanese bank.  \par \par \uc2 At 0345 GMT, it was trading at 106.11 yen, flat on its late New York level.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese government officials maintained that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed and that they would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as necessary to ensure market stability.  \par \par \uc2 But both Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, declined to comment on speculation about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen was at 128.64 per euro , up slightly from 128.77 in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was on the defensive against the dollar, trading at around $1.2139 , pulling back from a high of $1.2202 on Thursday on concerns that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut interest rates in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was trading at 192.20 yen , slightly up from 191.79 in New York, although still weak compared to levels seen earlier this month. The pound has dropped about eight percent against the yen in less than three weeks.  \par \par \uc2 A general sell-off in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sparked by comments from Bank of England Governor Mervyn King on Thursday that a strong pound was hurting British exporters, although the possibility of a rate rise in the near future could lend support.  \par \par \uc2 POSITIVE DATA  \par \par \uc2 Japan's core nationwide consumer price index (CPI) was unchanged in February from a year earlier - slightly better news on deflation than the market had expected, with economists forecasting a 0.1 percent decline.  \par \par \uc2 Other data showed that spending by Japanese households rose for the fourth consecutive month, suggesting Japan's export-led recovery may be trickling down to consumers.  \par \par \uc2 It was the latest upbeat news on the Japanese economy, following data earlier this month that showed Japan's economy grew at an annualised 6.4 percent in October-December, outpacing the 4.1 percent growth in the United States in the same quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "The rally in Japanese stocks is sparking yen-buying by foreign investors and this morning's CPI and other data may be viewed as another yen-positive factor," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group at UFJ Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Traders generally doubted that the yen would break through the all-important 105 per dollar level before the end of the Japanese fiscal year next Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 But some said it would cross the line in time if the Japanese authorities saw less need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rosier economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei share average surged to a 22-month high on Friday morning on rising economic optimism, helped by foreign investors, who snapped up a record 1.1483 trillion yen ($11 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week ended March 19.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040326e03q000uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
