\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Spot gold steadies in Asia, TOCOM firms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     SINGAPORE, March 26 (Reuters) - Spot gold steadied in Asia\par on Friday after earlier gains as the euro failed to make much\par headway against the dollar, although rising global tension was\par lending support for the safe-haven metal, dealers said.\par     Gold        was fetching $416.75/417.50 an ounce by 0457\par GMT after trading as high as $417.50 on renewed buying interest\par from Japanese investors. That compared with New York's last\par quoted level of 416.50/417.25.\par  \par     Dealers pegged key support at $412 and resistance at $424\par -- a level last seen in mid-January.\par     "It seems nobody wants to go short too much because\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 anything could happen at the weekend after (Israel) killed  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Hamas spiritual leader," said Ronald Leung, director of Lee  \par \par \uc2 Cheong Gold Dealers Ltd in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 "Who knows what will happen next," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said interest in gold had increased this week after  \par \par \uc2 Monday's assassination of Hamas leader Sheikh Ahmed Yassin  \par \par \uc2 because of fears of reprisals.  \par \par \uc2 And even though movements of the dollar against other  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  still largely determine gold's direction, the  \par \par \uc2 precious metal has become more sensitive to global tension  \par \par \uc2 following the recent bombings in Madrid.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was on the defensive against the dollar, at around  \par \par \uc2 $1.2144 , pulling back from a high of $1.2202 on Thursday  \par \par \uc2 on concerns the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut interest rates  \par \par \uc2 soon.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     A stronger euro makes dollar-priced gold a bargain for\par Europeans.\par     Tokyo gold futures defied the yen's appreciation against\par the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which normally induces heavy profit-taking.\par     The benchmark February 2005 gold contract         on the\par yen-based Tokyo Commodity Exchange (TOCOM) rose seven yen per\par gram to 1,422 yen.\par     "There was some buying in TOCOM by trading houses but it's\par not significant," said one Tokyo dealer.\par     The dollar fell to a five-week low against the yen\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 after further positive news on the Japanese economy but  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 steadied by midsession after suspected Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In other precious metals, palladium fell to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $282.50/287.50 an ounce from New York's last quoted level of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $283/288.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Platinum was quoted at $910/914 an ounce versus  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 $908/912 in New York, and silver stood at $7.56/7.58 an  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ounce, versus $7.58/7.60 last quoted in the U.S. market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040326e03q000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
