\par\par \b \uc2 Yen surges to 5-week high against dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 02:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 26 (Reuters) - The yen surged to a five-week high against the dollar on Friday, bolstered by strong foreign buying of Japanese stocks and increasing optimism on the outlook for Japan's economy.   \par \par \uc2  "The rally in Japanese stocks is sparking yen-buying by foreign investors and this morning's CPI and other data may be viewed as another yen-positive factor," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing group at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's core nationwide consumer price index (CPI) was unchanged in February from a year earlier - slightly better news on deflation than the market had expected, with economists forecasting a 0.1 percent decline.   \par \par \uc2  Other data showed that spending by Japanese households rose for the fourth consecutive month, suggesting Japan's export-led recovery may be trickling down to consumers.   \par \par \uc2  It was the latest upbeat news on the Japanese economy, following data earlier this month that showed Japan's economy grew at an annualised 6.4 percent in October-December, outpacing the 4.1 percent growth in the United States in the same quarter.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell as low as 105.63 yen its lowest since February 18 and well below highs of above 112 hit earlier this month. It was around 106.05 yen at 0130 GMT, roughly where it was in late New York trade on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at 128.56 per euro slightly stronger than 128.71 in late New York trade.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was on the defensive against the dollar, trading at around $1.2135 pulling back from a high of $1.2202 on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that fighting unemployment was an urgent priority for the euro zone economy, adding to recent statements that many see as pointing to a rate cut by the ECB in the near term.   \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei share average surged to a 22-month high on Friday morning on rising economic optimism, helped along by foreign investors, who snapped up a record 1.1483 trillion yen ($11 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week ended March 19.   \par \par \uc2  Worries about the sluggish euro zone economy and slow jobs growth in the United States point to further gains in the yen, analysts say, but in the short term the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities could keep yen bulls in check.   \par \par \uc2  "There's still a reasonable chance the BOJ will step in to defend the 105.50 yen mark, because a fall through that would probably take us very quickly down to 105 and below," said Sahara.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, declined to comment on Friday on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market recently, and also said that the Group of Seven's joint statement issued last month was "sufficiently clear".   \par \par \uc2  That statement said that excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was undesirable, but it also called for exchange rate flexibility.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040326e03q0005w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
