\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: Next-FY Budget Will Boost Econ Recovery  \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 09:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday steady implementation of the newly enacted budget for next fiscal year will help solidify the nation's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy is finally starting to show bright signs," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 "We will work to steadily implement next fiscal year's budget to make sure (the recovery) becomes firm," he told reporters after Japan's Upper House of Parliament Friday enacted an Y82.111-trillion budget bill for the fiscal year starting April 1.  \par \par \uc2 The budget requires a further huge issuance of government debt but keeps spending roughly in line with the current fiscal year. It will also aid the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to stem the yen's rise against the dollar, boosting by Y40 trillion the amount the MOF can borrow on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account to finance \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ 26-03-04 0853GMT ]  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy has been showing signs of steady improvement, as reflected in recent gains in Tokyo share prices and strong economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Friday, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average closed at a new 21-month high as investors went after a wider range of economy-sensitive stocks. The index rose 239.74 points, or 2.1%, to 11770.65, closing higher for the third consecutive session. It was its highest close since June 3, 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Data showing spending by Japanese wage earners jumped 6.9% in February in real terms from a year earlier, for the fourth consecutive month of gains, also suggested growing strength in Japan's long-stagnant domestic demand. The rise was the largest since October 1982, although government officials said most of the gains were due to 2004 being a leap year.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, who in describing the fledgling economic recovery is fond of comparing it to a "chick trying to break out of a thick shell," said that economic conditions have improved in some areas of Japan - so it could be said that "a healthy chick has hatched and is growing."  \par \par \uc2 But he added that the "shell" remains thick in other areas of the country and that the recovery remains "spotty."  \par \par \uc2 "We will continue our efforts" to bring about a full economic recovery, Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie [ 26-03-04 0920GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040326e03q000dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
