\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Consumption Picks Up, Deflation Easing  \b0\par\par\uc2 932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      (Adds Finance Minister's comments, analysts' comments, market reaction) \par  \par      By Leika Kihara and Iain McDonald \par      Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese domestic consumption continued to pick up last month and deflation pressures eased, according to data issued Friday, signaling the country's economic recovery is broadening to all sectors.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Retail sales rose 0.9% in February from a year ago, marking the second consecutive monthly rise. Spending by wage earners jumped 6.9%, the largest on-year increase since October 1982, although half of that rise was due to an extra leap-year day in February this year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that after removing the leap-year effect the data still show that previously weak consumption is growing, producing a broader economic recovery, which initially was dependent on rising exports and business investment.  \par \par \uc2 "This is good news for a Japan waiting on a consumer recovery," said Takuji Aida, senior economist at Merrill Lynch in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The strong data underscore the government's upbeat view on consumer spending. In February's monthly report, it upgraded its view on consumption, saying it is "showing signs of picking up."  \par \par \uc2 The figures also showed that Japan's nationwide core consumer price index, which the Bank of Japan follows in setting monetary policy, was flat in February from a year earlier. The core index for the Tokyo metropolitan area was down 0.1% in March from a year ago, a slightly smaller fall than the 0.2% drop expected by economists.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par         Stock Prices, Yen Up On Data; Bonds Lower \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong data boosted stock prices and pushed prices of Japanese government bonds lower. The yen rose against the dollar shortly after the data was issued, apparently on bids by non-Japanese players boosting exposure to Japanese shares. But the yen later shed most of the gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0518 GMT, the Nikkei 225 Stock Average was up 1.8% at 11742.59. June 10-year JGB futures were 0.75 lower at 138.29. The dollar was quoted at about Y106.10 after dipping to a low of Y105.63.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data has boosted expectations "that we are pretty close to getting out of deflation," said Susumu Kato, chief fixed-income strategist at Lehman Brothers. "Overall, this week's numbers suggest the further subsiding of deflation pressure."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The upbeat data also boosted expectations that non-Japanese investment funds will continue to flow into Japan's equity market, putting upward pressure on the yen against other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  along the way.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The strength in consumption is real and the medium-term outlook for the economy looks pretty good," making Japanese shares "look even more like a good bargain" to non-Japanese investors, said Tohru Sasaki, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank in Tokyo.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That could make it harder for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to prevent yen rises from hurting the economy, he added. "If the economy is good, the MOF won't have any reason to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Sasaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities maintained they were keeping their guard up against an unwelcome yen rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki declined to comment on whether the yen's current uptrend was reflecting Japan's economic recovery. But he said Japan is carefully watching the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and underlying economic conditions that affect it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par         Government: Retail Sales Picking Up \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the most accurate guide on consumption, J.P. Morgan economist Ryo Hino said seasonally adjusted retail sales are best as these figures remove the impact of the leap year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On this measure, retail sales rose 1.7% in February from January, he said. "As it stands, retail sales are running 3.9% above fourth quarter," which is the highest growth rate since the first quarter of 1997, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sales at department stores were especially solid, growing 2.3% in February from a year ago after adjusting for a change in the number of stores, the highest since November 2001, according to the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Sales of clothing were strong" in department stores, the official said. "Bargain sales of winter clothes went well and spring clothes sold well due to warm weather."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the official said the ministry hasn't changed its view on retail sales, saying the sales "are showing signs of picking up," because the change in increase wasn't as large as January when retail sales rose 1.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A breakdown of the wage earners' spending figures show people are spending more on leisure items as consumers loosen their purse strings, reflecting a brighter economic outlook.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spending on eating out jumped 11.1% and that on transport and telecommunications rose 19.0%, reflecting greater spending on cars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But an official briefing on the CPI data was cautious about the price outlook, saying the trend in Tokyo prices "isn't that strong."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, prices of durable goods such as personal computers continued to fall, with overall downward pressure on prices offset only by one-time factors like higher medical charges and a rise in rice prices due to a poor harvest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the data show that deflationary pressures have eased. The Tokyo core CPI fell at a pace of 0.3% in the fiscal year through March, slower than a 0.9% drop the previous year and a 1.1% fall in fiscal 2001.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leika Kihara and Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; Leika.kihara@dowjones.com (Michiyo Seki and Asako Tanabe contributed to this article.) [ 26-03-04 0520GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040326e03q0005z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
