\par\par \b \uc2 Yen up on Japan optimism, euro falls on rate talk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 25 (Reuters) - The yen marched higher across the board on Thursday on more confidence about Japan's economy, contrasting with a dim outlook in the euro-zone that had investors betting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 Optimism over Japan's recovery got another boost on Wednesday after ratings agency Standard & Poor's revised up its outlook on Japan to stable from negative.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. hedge funds, the major players in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, are certainly becoming yen bulls now and euro bearish, and they are betting that Japan's recovery is based on structural improvements," said Kiichi Murashima, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 Recent market enthusiasm for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stemmed in part from news earlier in the month that Japan's economy grew at an annualised 6.4 percent in October-December, outpacing the 4.1 percent in the United States in the same quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The yen broke above the psychologically important 106 yen level in Tokyo, rising to a five-week high around 105.80 per dollar  \par \par \uc2 Against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen surged to a four-month high of around 128.35 per euro before trimming gains to 128.60.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling also fell versus the yen, trading at 193.50 yen down half a yen from the late New York level.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers noted that global funds keep flowing into Japan, with foreigners buying a record 1.1483 trillion yen ($11 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week ended March 19.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also seen getting a minor boost from better-than-expected Japanese trade balance data for February, with the surplus rising 51.7 percent from a year earlier.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  BARRIER  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the yen looked set to strengthen further but Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or market speculation about such action, was almost the only factor in its way.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged, reminding the market yet again that it was ready to act.  \par \par \uc2 Market sources said that the Japanese authorities, acting through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, selling the yen and buying dollars.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said Japan was going to find it difficult to stop the yen's advance.  \par \par \uc2 "As the euro is weakening, Japan won't be able to stop the yen's rise just by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar/yen," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen is strong across the cross trades but \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to get agreements from Europeans for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in crosses especially when the ECB is leaning towards cutting rates," he said.  \par \par \uc2 RATE-CUT SPECULATION  \par \par \uc2 The euro had its own reasons for spiraling lower after comments from senior ECB officials raised investor speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates, perhaps as soon as April.  \par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, the euro continued to grind down to a three-week low of $1.2105 in Tokyo before trimming looses to trade at $1.2131/36.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expected the possibility of a ECB rate cut would continue to weigh on the euro through next week when the ECB meets, but added that the overall dollar-selling trend had yet to subside.  \par \par \uc2 "The possibility of the rate cut is putting pressure on the euro, but the view that the dollar will weaken further hasn't changed," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, traders were awaiting testimony from Bank of England Governor Mervyn King at 0930 GMT, as well as U.S. initial jobless claims data due at 1330 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Also awaited were comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan at 1400 GMT, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow at 1500 GMT, as well as a batch of Fed officials, including New York Fed President Timothy Geithner and St. Louis President, William Poole.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040325e03p001cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
