\par\par \b \uc2 Yen up on Japan optimism, euro down on rate talk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, March 25 (Reuters) - The yen powered higher across the board on Thursday on more confidence about Japan's economy, contrasting with a dim outlook in the euro-zone that had investors betting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 Optimism over Japan's recovery got another boost on Wednesday after international ratings agency Standard & Poor's revised up its outlook on the economy to stable from negative.  \par \par \uc2 The yen broke above the psychologically important 106 yen level, rising to a five-week high of 105.91 per dollar before easing back to around 106.10 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. hedge funds, the major players in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, are certainly becoming yen bulls now and euro bearish, and they are betting that Japan's recovery is based on structural improvements," said Kiichi Murashima, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 Global funds are still flowing into Japan, with foreigners buying a record 1.1483 trillion yen ($11 billion) of Japanese stocks in the week ended March 19.  \par \par \uc2 The yen also got a boost from better-than-expected Japanese trade balance data for February, with the surplus rising 51.7 percent from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was almost the only factor dampening the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Market sources said that the Japanese authorities, acting through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday, selling the yen and buying dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, warned again that Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged and in line with a stance agreed by the Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 issued a statement at the end of its meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, in early February saying excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was undesirable, although it also called for exchange rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 EURO WEAKENS FURTHER  \par \par \uc2 The euro spiraled lower on Wednesday after comments from senior ECB officials raised investor speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates, perhaps as soon as April, which would weaken the euro.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet told German business newspaper Handelsblatt late on Tuesday that the ECB would reassess its outlook for gradual euro zone recovery if consumer spending failed to pick up.  \par \par \uc2 ECB council member Guy Quaden said in an interview with French newspaper Le Monde the bank has some room to manoeuvre if needed, even though its official 2 percent rate is "extremely low".  \par \par \uc2 The euro continued to grind down to around $1.2105 in Tokyo before trimming looses to trade at $1.2130/34.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro dipped to a four-month low around 128.35 yen extending overseas losses.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expected the possibility of a ECB rate cut would continue to weigh on the euro through next week when the ECB meets, but added that the overall dollar-selling trend had yet to subside.  \par \par \uc2 "The possibility of the rate cut is putting pressure on the euro, but the view that the dollar will weaken further hasn't changed," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, traders were awaiting testimony from Bank of England Governor Mervyn King later in the day for more hints about a possible rate hike, as well as U.S. initial jobless claims and comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040325e03p000jj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
