\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar up Against European Rivals as Rate Talk Hits Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar surged against its major European rivals Wednesday as the euro plunged across the board on mounting speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower interest rates, but lost ground against a rampant yen.  \par \par \uc2 Comments from ECB chief Jean-Claude Trichet and Belgian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Guy Quaden over the last 24 hours are fueling speculation the ECB will soon deliver a growth-boosting rate cut. This sent the euro sharply lower, particularly against the yen. For its part, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened across the board after ratings agency Standard & Poor's upgraded its outlook for Japan's economy.  \par \par \uc2 This put the dollar, which largely ignored solid U.S. economic data and news an explosives device was discovered on a French railway line Wednesday, under heavy pressure against the yen. Dealers noted an apparent lack of dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities, but the euro's crumble allowed the greenback to soar against the main European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted huge losses, crashing through key technical support and the bottom of recent ranges around $1.2160 against the dollar and Y130.00 against the yen. It slumped around three full yen on the day to a fresh four-month low of Y128.62.  \par \par \uc2 "The same sentiment is driving down interest rates (forecasts) and the euro," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago, noting the big moves in interest rate futures markets Wednesday. Euribor futures are pricing in a 25% chance the ECB will cut its benchmark refinancing rate of 2% by 25 basis points in April, he said. Just three weeks ago, they had indicated the ECB's next move on rates this year would be a hike.  \par \par \uc2 "The ECB is preparing the ground for a rate cut, I really believe that," said Michael Klawitter, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at WestLB in London. A cut some time in the second quarter, or even the threat of a cut, could drive the euro down to around $1.1750 by the end of June, he said, with the risks skewed to an even lower rate before the unit rebounds in the second half.  \par \par \uc2 In late trading in New York, the euro was at $1.2125, around two cents weaker than $1.2325 in New York late Tuesday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled against the yen to 128.80 yen from 131.60 yen late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 That yen strength - the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit multi-month highs against the Australian dollar, Swiss franc and sterling - pushed the dollar down to a new one-month low of 106.05 yen. The dollar exited New York trading at 106.25 yen compared with 106.74 yen late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fared much better against the Swiss franc, strengthening to 1.2770 franc from 1.2585 franc, while sterling slid to $1.8238 from $1.8495 late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar barely reacted to the two pieces of U.S. economic data which both beat economists' expectations. Durable goods orders in February rose 2.5% compared with forecasts of a 1.5% rise, while new home sales last month rose 5.8% to a seasonally adjusted 1.163 million. Economists had predicted a total of 1.106 million.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the focus Wednesday was largely on whether or not the ECB will put its money where its mouth increasingly appears to be and actually lower rates.  \par \par \uc2 That, of course remains to be seen. Many observers doubt the ECB will follow up its tough talking with tough action. The ECB is charged with controlling inflation over the medium term and has no explicit mandate to foster economic growth through monetary policy. In addition, the young institution - the euro is barely five years old - has consistently fought hard to build up its credibility and maintain its independence from the bloc's politicians. And with oil prices stubbornly high and showing few signs of softening, the ECB will be fearful of the specter on the horizon of inflation despite current low levels.  \par \par \uc2 "I'll believe it when I see it," said Axel Merk, President of Merk Investments, a portfolio manager based in Palo Alto, Calif with around $50 million in assets under management.  \par \par \uc2 But the prospect of lower rates is proving sufficient in itself to encourage investors to dump euros. And a weaker euro is exactly what policy makers in the euro zone would welcome, as it eases the squeeze on the region's exports and general profitability and competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's "sherpa," or representative, on Group of Seven issues, Alfred Tacke, said Wednesday the euro's current exchange rate with the dollar "is approaching a fairer level."  \par \par \uc2 Germany's Economy Minister Hans Eichel said the recent G7 meeting in Florida and its statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  developments has had some impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which have since "calmed down to some degree."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the yen roared higher across the board Wednesday after S&P upgraded Japan's economic outlook to "stable" from "negative". This "reinforces the view that (Japan's) economic recovery is continuing," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Amid such an environment, analysts say, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen in a bid to stoke export-led growth. Investors might be wary of official Japanese support for the dollar, however, if the greenback slips even further and 105.00 yen comes into view.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 03-24-04 1658ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040324e03o00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
