\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Slightly Higher In Tokyo After Baghdad Bomb  \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Prices of U.S. Treasurys were slightly higher after a bombing in Baghdad encouraged flight-to-quality buying in an overall quiet trading session, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 A rocket struck the Sheraton Hotel in Iraq's capital, but no casualties were reported.  \par \par \uc2 At 0600 GMT, 10-year notes were up 5/32 at 102 16/32 to yield 3.70%, while 30-year bonds were 10/32 higher at 111 2/32 to yield 4.65%.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the small rise in prices, some said the gains weren't likely to be extended significantly given that prices are already high. On top of that, trading activity is slow ahead of Japan's fiscal year-end on March 31 and the next set of U.S. labor data due April 2.  \par \par \uc2 "Dealers and investors are thinking it's too high to buy," said Kazuaki Oh'e, executive director of capital markets at CIBC in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Surveys are showing that expectations of profits from new bond purchases are lower than last week, while expectations for losses are higher," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Later Wednesday, the Treasury will auction $26 billion of two-year notes. Oh'e said demand may not be strong due to a lack of funds flowing into the sector from Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 In particular, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited its dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks so won't have a large surplus of dollars it needs to invest, he added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of Treasurys usually focus on the short-end of the market.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Following are prices around 0600 GMT: \par  \par    COUPON  MATURITY   PRICE     CHANGE    YIELD   CHANGE \par    1 5/8%   2-year   100 10/32  up  1/32   1.46%  -2 BP \par    2 1/4%   3-year   101  3/32  up  2/32   1.85%  -3 BP \par    2 5/8%   5-year    99 28/32  up  3/32   2.65%  -2 BP \par    4    %  10-year   102 16/32  up  5/32   3.70%  -1 BP \par    5 3/8%  30-year   111  2/32  up 10/32   4.65%  -2 BP \par    2-10-Yr Yield Spread: 223 BPs Vs 223 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the Chicago Board of Trade, the June bond futures contract was up 7/32 at 116 1/32 from the previous session. The most heavily traded March 2005 Eurodollar contract on the Singapore Exchange was at 98.230, unchanged from the previous session.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2941; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie [ 24-03-04 0624GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1079 4041  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040324e03o0006v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
