\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar defensive, security and rate concerns weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 23 (Reuters) - The dollar held steady against the euro and remained close to one-month lows on the yen and Swiss franc on Tuesday as security concerns and still-fading prospects of dollar-boosting U.S. rate rises weighed it down.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said security had become a more acute issue for the dollar after Israel killed the spiritual leader of the Hamas militant group, and as Pakistani troops remained locked in battle with a group of suspected al Qaeda fighters.   \par \par \uc2  Dovish comments by Richmond Federal Reserve chief Alfred Broaddus and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow on Monday also highlighted subdued U.S. labour markets and inflation, signalling the Fed was likely to be patient in raising rates.   \par \par \uc2  "There is a growing sense within the market that the U.S. stock market has priced in all the good news. Relatively dovish comments from the Fed underpinned the view that U.S. rates are not going to rise soon, said Mark McFarland, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in London.   \par \par \uc2  "So from a yield perspective there isn't much out there to underpin the dollar. Investors are shying away from dollar assets after the events in the Middle East."   \par \par \uc2  At 0850 GMT, the dollar traded flat on the day at $1.2331 per euro, retaining most of the previous day's losses, after earlier easing as far as $1.2369.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded 0.18 percent down on the day at 1.2547 Swiss francs after earlier approaching one-month lows below 1.2520.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Swiss franc rose to three-month highs against the euro at 1.5479.   \par \par \uc2  WOBBLY SENTIMENT   \par \par \uc2  The dollar tends to suffer in periods of security concern with U.S. interests viewed as prime targets for attack.   \par \par \uc2  In addition, heightened risk aversion among investors tends to result in funds returning home, leaving the dollar exposed to downward pressure resulting from the U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Fed's Broaddus told Reuters in an interview on Monday there was plenty of slack in the U.S. economy and it would take clear evidence of tighter labour markets and higher core inflation to warrant higher interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  This week is relatively light in U.S. data, with the keenly watched non-farm payrolls due next week.   \par \par \uc2  "The output recovery has got to stay intact. If that fizzles out before you get a kick-through in employment that's going to be bad news for the equity market and bad news for the dollar," said Ian Gunner, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mellon Financial Corp in London.   \par \par \uc2  EYEING THE BOJ   \par \par \uc2  Investors remain wary about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which trapped the dollar in a tight range around 106.72 yen, not far above a one-month low around 106.36 set last week.   \par \par \uc2  "It certainly does feel as if Japanese authorities will continue to exert control over the yen until the fiscal year ends," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac in Sydney.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese corporations close their books for the 2003/04 fiscal year on March 31, and a weaker yen helps boost the value of their overseas sales when repatriated.   \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday there was no change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and that the authorities would continue to act as needed in the market.   \par \par \uc2  Speculation is building that the recent flow of upbeat news on Japan's economy will make it hard for Tokyo to justify a continuation of its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - especially ahead of a Washington meeting of the Group of Seven major developed countries in late April.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's tertiary sector index, a measure of activity in the services sector, rose 2.6 percent in January from the previous month to 110.7, a record high, while the all-industries index, which covers a broader range of activity, also rose by 2.6 percent.   \par \par \uc2  The figures were seen as more evidence that the country's export-led recovery was broadening out to the domestic economy.   \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the data timetable is also thin this week but the respected Ifo index of German business sentiment due on Friday will give indications of how well the economy is holding up to recent euro strength.   \par \par \uc2  Italian consumer sentiment edged up to 100.8 in March compared with 100 in February, according to research group ISAE.   \par \par \uc2  Some 87 percent of the survey was carried out before the bombings in Madrid nearly two weeks ago and ISAE said morale among those questioned after the attacks was sharply lower.   \par \par \uc2  January euro zone combined direct and portfolio outflows totalled 22.4 billion euros, up from an outflow of 9.8 billion in December.   \par \par \uc2  A host of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials make speeches during the day while U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow makes two appearances, at 1700 GMT and 1930 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n0019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
