\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar range-bound, little direction.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 22:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 23 (Reuters) - The dollar traded in a tight range on Tuesday, with investors looking for direction as global security concerns weighed on the market.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had gained modestly in early trading, recovering somewhat from the previous session's fall on concerns over the repercussions of Israel's killing the spiritual leader of the militant group Hamas.  \par \par \uc2 But by late afternoon in New York, the dollar was rangebound amid a lack of news to move it one way or another.  \par \par \uc2 "People are looking for something that is going to send them in a new direction. The market continues to trade in a narrower and narrower band," said John Hazelton, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at PNC Bank in Pittsburgh. "It will break one day ... it's inevitable. So for now, it's wait and see."  \par \par \uc2 The euro traded at $1.2320 down 0.1 percent against the dollar from Monday's New York close, according to Reuters data. The euro had been as high as $1.2369 and as low as $1.2266.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems that it's range-trading at the moment. Until we take out $1.2450 or $1.2150 on the downside (in euro/dollar) it appears that we are going to bounce around these ranges," said Grant Wilson, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against the Swiss franc to trade at 1.2595 francs . Sterling was steady at $1.8477 , having traded as low as $1.8407.  \par \par \uc2 Investors remained wary about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping the dollar near the unchanged mark on the day at 106.75 yen .  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks bounced in and out of positive territory after hitting 2004 lows on Monday. The Dow Jones industrial average slipped 0.01 percent in late trading.  \par \par \uc2 "Let's remember, everyone has one eye on the screens waiting for the next shoe to drop on terrorism. But for the most part, the euro has been rangebound for the past two to four weeks and continues to be so," said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 EURO NEGATIVES  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet was quoted by German business daily Handelsblatt on Tuesday as saying that the ECB would reassess the economic situation should its scenario of stronger consumption and rising demand not materialize.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the global day, the ECB reported that money flowed out of the euro zone in January at nearly double the average pace of the preceding six months. Outflows of direct and portfolio investment totaled 22.4 billion euros against a revised 9.8 billion in December, a negative for the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The strong outflows came as the euro zone posted a net current account deficit in January of 5.3 billion euros, versus a surplus in December.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about euro zone weakness have kept the euro in check, with markets pricing in about a 40 percent chance of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut by June, even though official rhetoric has not reflected this.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n0035z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
