\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up as stocks rise, security concerns wane.  \b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous LONDON  \par \par \uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 23 (Reuters) - The dollar made broad but modest gains on Tuesday, recouping earlier losses against the euro and Swiss franc, as most major equity markets rebounded from Monday's losses and European economic data proved weak.  \par \par \uc2 Heightened security concerns that have weighed on the dollar waned a day after Israel killed Sheikh Ahmed Yassin, the spiritual leader of the militant group Hamas, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Stocks look like they have stabilized and that is somewhat supportive of the dollar and there is nothing new on the terrorism front this morning, so that is also a positive for the dollar," said Hugh Walsh, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Let's remember, everyone has one eye on the screens waiting for the next shoe to drop on terrorism. But for the most part, the euro has been rangebound for the past two to four weeks and continues to be so," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Pakistan's siege of suspected al Qaeda fighters near the Afghan border, in addition to Yassin's death, have kept market risk aversion at the fore less than two weeks after the Madrid train bombings renewed security concerns in Europe.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to $1.2290, off 0.37 percent from Monday's New York close, according to Reuters data. Traders put the range roughly between $1.2150 and $1.2450.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed half a percent to 1.2630 Swiss francs after earlier approaching one-month lows below 1.2520.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc earlier rose to three-month highs against the euro at 1.5477 francs before easing back to 1.5519 francs, up slightly on the day..  \par \par \uc2 Investors remained wary about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping the dollar close to break-even on the day at 106.82 yen, up slightly on the day but still only about half a yen above last week's one-month low of 106.36 yen.  \par \par \uc2 After hitting 2004 lows in recent days, U.S. stocks recovered some ground in early New York trade. The Dow Jones industrial average and S&P 500 stock index were up nearly half a percentage point, while the Nasdaq Composite index nearly notched a 1 percent gain.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a bit of positive equity sentiment creeping in," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people are wondering whether the killing of Yassin matters for markets. It does a bit but not as much as it seemed to matter yesterday," he said.  \par \par \uc2 EURO NEGATIVES  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported money flowed out of the euro zone in January at nearly double the average pace of the preceding six months. Outflows of direct and portfolio investment totaled 22.4 billion euros against a revised 9.8 billion in December, a negative for the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The strong outflows came as the euro zone posted a net current account deficit in January of 5.3 billion euros, versus a surplus in December.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about euro zone weakness have kept the euro in check, with markets pricing in about a 40 percent chance of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut by June, even though official rhetoric has not reflected this.  \par \par \uc2 ECB VOICE  \par \par \uc2 ECB Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell said growth in the euro zone would pick up speed with little threat from inflation.  \par \par \uc2 She said recent data had been mixed but did not expect that last month's deadly bombings in Madrid would have a lasting impact on economic sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 The cloud hanging over the euro helped the dollar on the margins, especially after Richmond Federal Reserve chief Alfred Broaddus and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow signaled the Fed remained cautious about raising rates.  \par \par \uc2 Given economic data is sparse this week, investors are likely to tune in with more attention to policy makers, including U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, who is due to speak at 1700 GMT and then again at 1930 GMT on Tuesday. (Additional reporting by Burton Frierson and Charlotte Cooper in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n002ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
