\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar edges up vs euro, remains stuck in range.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 23 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed against the euro and Swiss franc, reversing earlier losses on Tuesday as European economic weakness and positive U.S. stock futures offset security concerns and fading U.S. rate hike expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Israel's killing on Monday of Hamas spiritual leader Ahmed Yassin, and Pakistan's siege of suspected al Qaeda fighters near the Afghan border kept risk aversion at the fore less than two weeks after the Madrid bombings sparked security worries.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve officials highlighted the subdued U.S. labour market overnight, denting dollar sentiment, but firming stock futures emerged on Tuesday to support the greenback while European interest rate doubts also dogged the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "There's still a threat that the ECB could cut rates that undermines the euro. The fundamentals in the U.S. have deteriorated - that undermines the dollar. So, it's trapped for the moment in a range," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets.  \par \par \uc2 "Because of the lack of economic direction and data at the moment the security issue has played a bit more of a role than it would normally. But if you look at the turning point for a lot of things it's the U.S. non-farm payrolls numbers, not anything else."  \par \par \uc2 At 1300 GMT, the dollar traded nearly half a percent firmer on the day at $1.2280 per euro but remained well within recent ranges after earlier easing as far as $1.2369.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose half a percent to 1.2630 Swiss francs after earlier approaching one-month lows below 1.2520.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc earlier rose to three-month highs against the euro at 1.5477 before easing back to the break-even mark around 1.5500.  \par \par \uc2 Investors remained wary about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which trapped the dollar in a tight range around half a yen above a one-month low of 106.36 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow makes two appearances, at 1700 GMT and 1930 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS SUPPORT  \par \par \uc2 The firmer tone for Wall Street futures came after stocks slid for the third session in a row to hit new lows for 2004 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a bit of positive equity sentiment creeping in," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people are wondering whether the killing of Yassin matters for markets. It does a bit but not as much as it seemed to matter yesterday."  \par \par \uc2 Euro sentiment took a hit after the ECB said money flowed out of the euro zone in January at nearly double the average pace of the last six months, with outflows of direct and portfolio investment totalling 22.4 billion euros in January.  \par \par \uc2 The strong outflows came as the euro zone posted a net current account deficit in January of 5.3 billion euros, versus a surplus in December.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about euro zone weakness have kept the euro in check, with markets pricing in about a 40 percent chance of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) rate cut by June, even though official rhetoric has not reflected this.  \par \par \uc2 ECB VOICE  \par \par \uc2 ECB Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell said growth in the euro zone would pick up speed with little threat from inflation.  \par \par \uc2 She said recent data had been mixed but did not expect that last month's deadly bombings in Madrid would have a lasting impact on economic sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 The cloud hanging over the euro helped the dollar on the margins, especially after Richmond Federal Reserve chief Alfred Broaddus and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow signalled the Fed remained cautious about raising rates.  \par \par \uc2 The comments hit a market already inclined to sell dollars over concerns U.S. interests were vulnerable to security threats.  \par \par \uc2 Broaddus told Reuters in an interview there was plenty of slack in the U.S. economy and it would take clear evidence of tighter labour markets and higher core inflation to warrant higher interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 This week's relatively light in U.S. data calendar, with markets mainly awaiting non-farm payrolls due next week, has exacerbated the effect of security concerns.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a dollar story that looks negative but a market that doesn't want to put risk on and a European story that is probably developing very quickly into a market that thinks the ECB may cut rates," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers in London.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n001u1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
