\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar caught in stocks/rates cross currents.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 23 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed against the euro but traded near one-month lows on the yen and Swiss franc on Tuesday, with security concerns and fading U.S. rate hike expectations counterbalanced by rising U.S. stock futures.  \par \par \uc2 Israel's killing on Monday of Hamas spiritual leader Ahmed Yassin, and Pakistan's siege of suspected al Qaeda fighters near the Afghan border kept risk aversion at the fore less than two weeks after the Madrid bombings sparked security worries.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve officials highlighted subdued U.S. price pressures late on Monday, further denting dollar sentiment before firming Wall Street stock futures supported the greenback during European trade.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a bit of positive equity sentiment creeping in," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people are wondering whether the killing of Yassin matters for markets. It does a bit but not as much as it seemed to matter yesterday."  \par \par \uc2 At 1120 GMT, the dollar traded a touch firmer on the day at $1.2312 per euro, but retained most of the previous day's losses after earlier easing as far as $1.2369.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged higher to 1.2588 Swiss francs after earlier approaching one-month lows below 1.2520.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Swiss franc rose to three-month highs against the euro at 1.5477.  \par \par \uc2 WOBBLY SENTIMENT  \par \par \uc2 Monday's comments by Richmond Federal Reserve chief Alfred Broaddus and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow signalled the Fed remained cautious about raising rates and hit a market already inclined to sell dollars over concerns U.S. interests were vulnerable to security threats.  \par \par \uc2 Broaddus told Reuters in an interview there was plenty of slack in the U.S. economy and it would take clear evidence of tighter labour markets and higher core inflation to warrant higher interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 This week's relatively light in U.S. data calendar, with markets mainly awaiting non-farm payrolls due next week, has exacerbated the effect of security concerns.  \par \par \uc2 However, worries about euro zone weakness have also kept the euro in check, with markets pricing in about a 40 percent chance of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) rate cut by June, even though official rhetoric has not reflected this.  \par \par \uc2 "We really aren't getting the follow through on the price action," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers in London.  \par \par \uc2 "It is because we are in a tug-of-war. It's a dollar story that looks negative but a market that doesn't want to put risk on and a European story that is probably developing very quickly into a market that thinks the ECB may cut rates."  \par \par \uc2 The ECB said on Tuesday that money flowed out of the euro zone in January at nearly double the average pace of the last six months, with outflows of direct and portfolio investment totalling 22.4 billion euros in January.  \par \par \uc2 The strong outflows came as the euro zone posted a net current account deficit in January of 5.3 billion euros, versus a surplus in December.  \par \par \uc2 ECB Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell said growth in the euro zone would pick up speed with little threat from inflation.  \par \par \uc2 She said recent data had been mixed but did not expect that last month's deadly bombings in Madrid would have a lasting impact on economic sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 EYEING THE BOJ  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors remain wary about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which trapped the dollar in a tight range around half a yen above a one-month low of 106.36 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese corporations close their books for the 2003/04 fiscal year on March 31, and a weaker yen helps boost the value of their overseas sales when repatriated.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday there was no change in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and that the authorities would continue to act as needed in the market.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040323e03n001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
