\par\par \b \uc2 US Treasuries firm as stocks slip, supply looms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 17:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 22 (Reuters) - Treasuries prices edged higher on Monday, breaking three sessions of losses, as geopolitical concerns and a sell-off in global stocks benefited safe-haven U.S. government debt.  \par \par \uc2 Equities slipped in part due to Israel's killing of the spiritual leader of the Hamas Islamic militant group, Sheikh Ahmed Yassin, in its highest profile assassination of more than three years of conflict.  \par \par \uc2 The killing stirred fears of reprisals against U.S. targets and boosted the traditional safe-haven investments like bonds, gold and the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 The safety bid could help attract demand for Treasury's auction of $26 billion in new two-years notes, scheduled for Wednesday. There have been worries buying by foreign central banks would taper off now that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 "The auction will be a good opportunity to see if the BOJ is still going to back up the truck and load it with securities," said Richard Gilhooly, fixed-income strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "We suspect that even if the eventual plan is to slow down or stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they have done enough recently that they will need to come for some two-year notes," he added.  \par \par \uc2 So far this year, offshore central banks have bought a whopping $100 billion in Treasury and agency debt.  \par \par \uc2 Late Monday morning, the benchmark 10-year note had firmed 5/32 in price, compressing its yield to 3.76 percent from 3.77 percent late on Friday. That remains well below the 4.10 percent level this time last month but up on the eight-month trough of 3.65 percent hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 "With the market having failed to penetrate 3.65 percent, there is room for a backup into the 3.80's, perhaps as far as 3.90 percent," said BNP's Gilhooly.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Five-year notes  were up 4/32 in price, taking yields to 2.71 percent\par from 2.74 percent on Friday. The 30-year bond             rose 6/32,\par leaving its yield at 4.70 percent from 4.71 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two-year note added 1/32, easing its yield down to 1.50 percent from 1.52 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There were no U.S. economic data due Monday to offer a distraction from security concerns, but two top Federal Reserve officials are appearing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders hope one or other may expand on last week's policy statement in which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surprised many by taking a slightly less bullish stance on the economy and jobs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's caution encouraged the market to further reduce the risk of a tightening this year and sparked a sizable rally in Treasuries.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both San Francisco President Robert Parry and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow appear at 1.00 p.m. (1800 GMT) though only the latter is giving a formal speech on the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That last time Moskow spoke in February he said the Fed could afford to be patient on policy but warned that interest rates would have to rise at some point.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040322e03m0021x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
