\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares sag, Taiwan tumbles after poll.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 22 (Reuters) - Asian stock markets fell on Monday, led by Taiwan's near-seven percent tumble after a razor-thin election win for the president prompted widespread protests and opposition calls for a recount.   \par \par \uc2  Falls on Wall Street on Friday contributed to Asia's weakness. Tokyo's Nikkei average dropped 0.87 percent in morning trade to 11,319.15, led by a 2.2 percent drop in auto maker Nissan Motor Co.   \par \par \uc2  Consumer electronics maker Sanyo Electric Co Ltd fell 5.7 percent after slashing its full-year net profit estimate by 88 percent on Friday. MSCI's index of Asian share markets outside Japan fell 1.94 percent.   \par \par \uc2  By 0230 GMT, Taiwan's TAIEX index had sunk 6.65 percent to 6,361.57. Shares in Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co (TSMC), the world's largest contract microchip maker, slumped by the market's daily seven percent limit.   \par \par \uc2  "Even without any election disputes, Taiwan stocks would have fallen due to the U.S. stock fall. The political instability intensified the selling," said Michael On, managing director of Beyond Asset Management which has $60 million under management.   \par \par \uc2  "But I believe solid fundamentals will provide strong support at around 6,200-6,300, especially as the National Stabilisation Fund has vowed to support the market."   \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar slipped 0.3 percent to T$33.353 per U.S. dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  promised to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index dropped 1.5 percent after a half-hour of trade on the Taiwan political tensions. Property and finance counters fell 1.8 and 1.2 percent respectively, more than the 1.1 percent fall in China-related shares.   \par \par \uc2  Stocks in Fujian, across the Strait from Taiwan, vastly under-performed a steady broader China market. Brokers said mainland investors had been counting on a victory by the more moderate opposition leader Lien Chan.   \par \par \uc2  Xiamen C&D Co and Xiamen International Trade Group Corp both dropped by over two percent.   \par \par \uc2  Malaysian stocks jumped 1.4 percent on the back of an unexpectedly strong win for Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi in national polls on Sunday.   \par \par \uc2  PLEDGING SUPPORT   \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to maintain exchange rate stability following the dispute over the outcome of its presidential election.   \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will enter the market to maintain the Taiwan dollar's dynamic stability," Economics Minister Lin Hsin-yi said on Sunday.   \par \par \uc2  The bank's stated policy is to allow the Taiwan dollar to follow market trends, but it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soften sharp moves.   \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's High Court will consider calls for a recount after President Chen Shui-bian was declared the winner by just 29,000 votes out of nearly 13 million cast.   \par \par \uc2  In contrast, Abdullah's win in Malaysia gave him a clear mandate to push through economic reforms and an anti-corruption drive that has been welcomed by investors, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR FIRMS   \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed against the yen, but traders anticipated more pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the beating it took from its Japanese counterpart last week.   \par \par \uc2  In the face of indications that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry was loosening its grip on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said they expected the dollar to grind lower soon, although some said it was probably too early to test the 105 yen level.   \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't be surprised if the dollar kept falling today, but I'm not sure the speed of its descent will continue. I'm not sure we'll be seeing 105 yen today," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 107.54 yen, up from 106.65 in late New York on Friday. The euro traded around 131.67 yen, a touch higher from Friday. The euro bought $1.2244, also not far from where it finished U.S. trade.   \par \par \uc2  Spot gold was unchanged from late U.S. levels in early Asian trade at 411.50, with traders expecting narrow ranges for the day. NYMEX crude futures edged lower in light profit-taking after prices touched a fresh 13-month peak late last week, with front-month April crude, which expires at the close of business on Monday, down 14 cents to $37.94 a barrel.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks were expected to stay volatile this week as investors keep a wary eye on security threats around the world and watch for hints on first-quarter profits.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.06 percent to 10,186.60 and the technology-laden Nasdaq Composite Index fell 1.12 percent, to 1,940.47. It was the second straight down week for both indices.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040322e03m000e8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
