\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Likely To Weaken In Turbulent Trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Australia and New Zealand Report\par \uc2 DJAN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Turbulent trading in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is likely to continue this week as market players grapple with the apparent change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a tense and unpredictable geopolitical environment.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar will likely weaken against the yen, although its losses could be limited. Opinions diverge as to how the dollar will trade against the euro. Some expect a return to more stable trading after the gyrations seen in recent weeks, but there doesn't appear to be a clear trend for the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to analysts. Late Friday afternoon, the dollar was trading around Y106.71, little changed from Y106.75 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.2275, well below $1.2390 late Thursday in New York. It was also lower against the yen at Y131.02 versus Y132.20. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar rose to CHF1.2694 from CHF1.2565, while sterling slid to $1.8309 from $1.8340.  \par \par \uc2 The uncertainties that overshadow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets suggest bold, directional moves aren't likely this week, analysts said. Trading is expected to remain volatile, however, with some potential for sharp intraday moves.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall, we've been in choppy trade since the end of February and we probably will remain in choppy trade until the first week of April," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston.  \par \par \uc2 The recent focus on geopolitical developments will likely remain firmly entrenched. The March 11 terrorist bombings in Madrid, last week's close pursuit of a possible top al-Qaida leader in Pakistan and a number of other events have put global conflict at the forefront in global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The apparent change in the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has also resulted in added uncertainty. The rules of the game in trading the yen against the U.S. dollar may have changed, but there isn't yet a clear outline of what the new rules are.  \par \par \uc2 Some extension of last week's sharp drop in the U.S. dollar against the yen is likely, but with uncertainty about how the Bank of Japan will react, there will be limits to how much lower the dollar can go, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Fear Factor Not Going Away \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped aside last week after earlier chasing the U.S. dollar higher in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, but could step back into the fray to support the U.S. dollar at any point.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think the BOJ is just going to go on vacation," Mazanec said. "The fear factor is not going to go away."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Y105.00 level is a "huge psychological level" that will tend to support the U.S. dollar, he said. "I don't know if the BOJ can wait until we get there. I'm a little bit surprised they let it get this far.".  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Doug Porter, senior economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns Inc. in Toronto, said a challenge of the Y105.00 level is likely, but said the Bank of Japan would likely defend it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Y111.00 area is likely to form the top of the U.S. dollar's trading range against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the week, Mazanec noted.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Japanese data reinforces the view the domestic economy is recovering, that will also help buttress the yen against the dollar, said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro will tend to stabilise against the U.S. dollar this week after volatile trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's going to be looking a little more stable than we've seen in the last week or two, where it has, on occasion, looked quite heavy," said Shaun Osborne, financial markets economist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the euro's going to be doing a bit better, but we won't necessarily be pushing the envelope of $1.2900, $1.3000," Osborne said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Merrill's Kasumovich said the euro will likely be confined to recent trading ranges against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're in a range, and it's not obvious to me what takes us out of that range," he said. The dollar will likely stay between $1.2140 and $1.2440 during the week, Kasumovich said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Scant U.S. Data During The Week \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While an end to the wave of euro selling by shorter-term speculative players may have taken some of the pressure off the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , its outlook may be clouded by the continuing aftermath of the Madrid bombings and the potential for erosion of European business and consumer confidence.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Clearly, the Madrid bombings have at least temporariliy hurt business confidence and consumer confidence in Eu