\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firms on euro profit-taking, al Qaeda news.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 623 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 16:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, dateline; previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 19 (Reuters) - The dollar rebounded on Friday, benefiting from profit-taking on the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which had gained when a security alert in Europe the day before prompted safe-haven buying.  \par \par \uc2 News that Pakistani troops had closed in on a group of Islamic militants possibly including Osama bin Laden's second in command Ayman al-Zawahri also helped push the dollar higher on Friday on a drop in geopolitical tensions, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered above this week's one-month low against the yen, with traders unwilling to test Japan's tolerance of a higher yen any further.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar firmed slightly against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-morning New York trade to 106.76 yen .  \par \par \uc2 The market was nervous about the limits of Japan's patience for a potentially export damaging rise in the yen against the dollar, after a two-yen gain on the greenback on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "I just think it's a consolidation of yesterday's losses. Specifically we had a big move up in the euro against the dollar yesterday," said Shaun Osborne, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto, explaining part of the reason for the dollar's recovery on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 By midmorning in New York, the euro was down 0.4 percent against the dollar to $1.2344 . The euro had a brief spike after news that Washington DC police are searching schools after a bomb threat made over the Internet.  \par \par \uc2 The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also down against the yen at 131.66 yen . Against the Swiss franc, the greenback was up 0.4 percent at 1.2621 francs . Sterling dropped slightly against the dollar to $1.8347 .  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS WARY OF AL QAEDA, JAPAN  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also got some bids as news that Pakistani troops have surrounded between 300 and 400 rebels, both foreign militants and their Pakistani tribal allies, in a battle near the border with Afghanistan.  \par \par \uc2 "We had a pretty substantial sell-off in the dollar yesterday and that was capped by stories out of Pakistan that a high level al Qaeda operative would be captured," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in London.  \par \par \uc2 "That gave a bit of support overnight and that's just fed through to today's trading. But the selloff in the dollar hasn't really reversed, it's just been trimmed so far," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The data schedule for Friday is thin and analysts said that meant the market would trade on technical levels, geopolitical events and any hints of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been conspicuous by its absence this week after months of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the dollar's downside against the yen was limited for now unless the market could take out 106.50 yen. But some analysts were not ruling out BoJ action in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we will still see the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out sharp moves. The ranges havesee a decent bounce in dollar/yen and it would still be in a range," said Scotia Capital's Osborne.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki insisted remarks he made earlier in the week about Japan acting in the markets only if necessary were nothing out of the ordinary.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were skeptical that a dollar blip higher in the Tokyo afternoon, which took it up to 107.30, stemmed from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. Some said it was possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been back in the market but at much reduced levels. (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040319e03j0021r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
