\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen pauses after rise as market ponders BOJ.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 19 (Reuters) - The yen held firm on Friday near the previous session's one-month highs on the dollar and seven-week peaks on the euro but markets braced for more gains as Japanese authorities gave no signs they would halt its rise.  \par \par \uc2 Markets cautiously probed the limits of Japan's tolerance for a potentially export damaging yen rise against the dollar, which also took a hit against the Swiss franc on Thursday on the back of security fears.  \par \par \uc2 A pause in economic data left focus on geopolitical factors again on Friday as Pakistani troops closed in on a group of militants possibly including Osama bin Laden's second in command, which could lend support to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The key feature today is that there is no real data out. That means the market is going to trade technically or react to geopolitical events," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "We are now getting to levels where the market is a little bit cautious that the pace of the yen move may bring the Bank of Japan back out again. Given the gains you would have made this week by being long yen, people are nervous about trying to chase it."  \par \par \uc2 At 0855 GMT, the yen traded at 106.85 per dollar, a touch higher from the previous New York close and near Thursday's peak around 106.36.  \par \par \uc2 It held steady at 132.26 per euro, down from Thursday's high of 130.73.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar firmed a touch on the day to $1.2373 per euro, having shed 1-1/2 cents on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 CALM AFTER THE STORM  \par \par \uc2 Traders likened the yen's pause to the calm after a storm, in the wake of its two-yen rise against the dollar on Thursday that came on growing suspicions the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) may be looking to cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We've had a big shock from MOF...as such we'll see further downward pressure (on the dollar) over the next month or so," said Jake Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank in Tokyo, noting that most long-dollar positions had been cleared out of the market.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were sceptical a dollar blip higher in the Tokyo afternoon, which took it up to 107.30, stemmed from yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, although some pointed to the possibility authorities had been acting in the market but at much reduced levels.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expected a possible run at the 105 level in the near term, although the authorities are seen by some as being keen to protect that level, at least until the end of March, when Japanese corporations close their books for fiscal 2003/04.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is going to try to drive the dollar lower. If it sticks at 105 yen, we'll know what the Finance Ministry's stance is," said Hideaki Furumaya, a manager at Trust and Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Fuelling speculation about a change in tone over Tokyo's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Mainichi newspaper reported the government planned to trim back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid growing signs of an economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers found themselves trying to quash suspicions of a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki insisted remarks he made earlier in the week about Japan acting in the markets only if necessary were nothing out of the ordinary.  \par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are stable and reflect fundamentals, we won't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 "Our basic policy is as it was before, that we will act appropriately when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are not reflecting fundamentals."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040319e03j00180\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
