\par\par \b \uc2 Yen calm after bull run, but more gains eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 07:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 19 (Reuters) - The yen eased off its bullish run against major rivals on Friday, but the market expected further gains as speculation mounted that the Japanese authorities may be stepping back from aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Traders likened the yen's pause to the calm after a storm, noting it was common for the market to stabilise in the wake of big fluctuations such as those seen in the dollar/yen this week.   \par \par \uc2  On growing suspicions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) may be looking to cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged by two yen on Thursday to one-month highs around 106.35 yen - about five percent above five-month lows plumbed less than two weeks ago.   \par \par \uc2  "We've had a big shock from MOF...as such we'll see further downward pressure (on the dollar) over the next month or so," said Jake Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank in Tokyo, noting that most long-dollar positions had been cleared out of the market.   \par \par \uc2  At 0610 GMT, the dollar was resting at 107.11 yen after briefly fighting back against the Japanese unit, pushing up to as high as 107.30 yen in early trade.   \par \par \uc2  Market opinion was split as to whether the blip up had been driven by market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although some traders pointed to the possibility that the Japanese authorities had been acting in the market all week, albeit at minimal levels.   \par \par \uc2  Still, they said the yen was poised for more gains as the market was intent on testing the Finance Ministry's resolve.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar would likely take a run at the 105 level in the near term, although the authorities are seen keen by some to protect that level, at least until the end of March, when Japanese corporations close their books for fiscal 2003/04.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is going to try to drive the dollar lower. If it sticks at 105 yen, we'll know what the Finance Ministry's stance is," said Hideaki Furumaya, a manager at Trust and Custody Services Bank.   \par \par \uc2  CHANGE IN TONE?   \par \par \uc2  Fueling speculation about a change in tone over Tokyo's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Mainichi newspaper reported that the government planned to trim back \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid growing signs of an economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese policymakers found themselves trying to quash suspicions of a change in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki insisted remarks he made earlier in the week about Japan acting in the markets only if necessary were nothing out of the ordinary.   \par \par \uc2  "There was nothing special about that comment, which was also a tautology. If \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are stable and reflect fundamentals, we won't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Tanigaki said.   \par \par \uc2  "Our basic policy is as it was before, that we will act appropriately when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are not reflecting fundamentals."   \par \par \uc2  But traders pointed out that the market was interested more in what government officials were - or were not - doing than what they were saying.   \par \par \uc2  "From flows in the market, it looks as if they've stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so they can say they haven't changed anything but they've spent $100 billion in February (on dollars) and it looks like they haven't bought any this week," said Barclays' Moore.   \par \par \uc2  "Actions speak louder than words and there hasn't been any action this week."   \par \par \uc2  The euro was sitting around 132.72 yen, up from late New York's 130.72 - its lowest since January 30.   \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro was at $1.2391 slightly above late U.S. levels.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also under pressure from global security concerns after a fake bomb was found on the Eurostar train that runs between Britain and France.   \par \par \uc2  The hoax earlier unsettled the market still jittery from last week's bombings in Madrid, which killed around 200 people.   \par \par \uc2  In addition, market attention was focused on a 0700 GMT deadline set by the Pakistan army for a group of militants to surrender. The group was thought to include Osama bin Laden's second-in-command.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040319e03j000xk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
