\par\par \b \uc2 Ex-MOF Sakakibara: MOF Mulling End Of Massive Yen Sales  \b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 10:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government is turning away from massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar against the yen, a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks to me that their strategy has changed," Eisuke Sakakibara said in an interview on CNBC Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara - known as "Mr. Yen" for the sway he held over exchange rates during his tenure as Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs - said sustained large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risked causing "problems in various markets," prompting the ministry to reconsider its staunch defense of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 He pointed to the unprecedented scale of Tokyo's recent yen selling: it spent Y10 trillion in the first two months of this year, an amount already almost half the record \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  total for all of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "This is unusual, highly unorthodox, massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so they've been thinking of exiting from this regime," he said. "It's very difficult to have a smooth exit, but they seem to be doing it at this moment."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has plunged roughly Y4 this week as the ministry, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has apparently withdrawn its bids for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At 0947 GMT, the dollar was changing hands at Y107.07. [ 19-03-04 0957GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara, now an economics professor at Keio University in Tokyo, said the yen is bound to strengthen further in the absence of Tokyo's efforts to stem its rise.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the strong recovery of the economy and given the strong Nikkei, some gradual strengthening of the yen will probably be inevitable," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that despite the dollar's steep decline against the yen this week, a drastic fall in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the state of the U.S. economy, which is still very strong, I do not see any sharp falls for the dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that it could be "a different story" if the U.S. job market continues to struggle in the coming months.  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara declined to predict the level at which the dollar would trade against the yen at the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if the strengthening yen was likely to push the dollar below the Y100 mark this year, Sakakibara said, "I do not think that either Japan or the U.S. wants the yen at that level."  \par \par \uc2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com [ 19-03-04 1031GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040319e03j000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
